WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:23.470
<v Ira Glass>A few years back, I gave a talk at Chautauqua, in upstate New York, which, if you don't know what that is, starting in the 1870s, it was this high-minded lecture circuit, like TED Talks, but before the internet or Public Radio before the invention of radio. With a big religious component, which is how I fell into this long conversation about the literal word of the Bible.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，我在纽约北部肖托夸做了一次演讲，如果你不知道那是什么，从19世纪70年代开始，那是一个高格调的演讲巡回活动，就像TED演讲，但在互联网出现之前，或者在广播发明之前的公共广播。它包含重要的宗教成分，这就是我陷入那场关于圣经字面意思的漫长谈话的原因。

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:33.970
<v Ira Glass>It happened because the staffer who drove me to the airport was a retired Methodist preacher and associate pastor of one of the churches in Chautauqua, named John Jackson. And we had this hour and a half long drive.
<v Ira Glass>那是由于送我去机场的工作人员是约翰·杰克逊，一位退休的卫理公会传教士，也是肖托夸教堂之一的副牧师。我们经历了一个半小时的车程。

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:53.450
<v Ira Glass>And somehow, we got onto how he doesn't believe that every word of the Bible is literally true, which, if you're a Methodist, that's no big deal. And John said, that as he gets older, the literal words of the Bible seem less important to him than the big picture-- love your neighbor as yourself, and love God above all.
<v Ira Glass>不知怎么的，我们谈到了他不相信圣经的每一个字都是字面真实，如果你是卫理公会教徒，那没什么大不了的。约翰说，随着他年龄增长，圣经的字面意思对他来说似乎没有大局观重要——爱人如己，爱神至上。

00:00:53.450 --> 00:01:03.750
<v Ira Glass>And I told John, I totally get the "love your neighbor as yourself" part of that. I can see how that can reshape just everything about how you treat others and really everything you do in your life in the world.
<v Ira Glass>我告诉约翰，我完全理解“爱人如己”那部分。我能看出这如何重塑你对待他人以及你在世上所做的一切。

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:19.040
<v Ira Glass>But I told him, like, I've never really understood, why is it important to love God above all? Do you know what I mean? If you do what God wants, and you try to be good-- you try to treat others right-- what difference does it make if you love God? What does God care?
<v Ira Glass>但我告诉他，我从没真正理解，为什么爱神至上很重要？你明白我的意思吗？如果你做了神想要的事，尝试做一个好人——尝试正确对待他人——爱不爱神又有什么区别呢？神在乎吗？

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:32.670
<v Ira Glass>And I got to ask John about a variation on this question, that I've wondered about for a couple of years, ever since the last time I was in synagogue. This was, I don't know, this was two or three years before this conversation. I hadn't been to synagogue in forever.
<v Ira Glass>我问了约翰关于这个问题的一个变体，我思考这个问题已经好几年了，自从上次我去犹太教堂以来。那大约是在这次谈话的两三年前。我已经很久没去过犹太教堂了。

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:49.830
<v Ira Glass>And it was the anniversary of my mom's death. And we're Jews, so you're supposed to go say Kaddish, this old prayer that's one of the central prayers in Judaism, at the anniversary of somebody's death. And so my dad and my stepmom and I were at one of the daily services that observant Jews go to every day in Baltimore, where I grew up.
<v Ira Glass>那是母亲逝世的周年纪念日。我们是犹太人，所以按照规定要在亲人逝世周年那天诵读卡迪什，这是犹太教核心祈祷文之一。所以我和父亲、继母参加了巴尔的摩（我成长的地方）虔诚的犹太人每天都会参加的日常仪式。

00:01:49.830 --> 00:02:25.580
<v Ira Glass>And I always liked going to synagogue as a kid. We went a lot. And so it was nice going back. I know all the Hebrew prayers by heart. And I don't know if this is good or bad, but not having sat in a synagogue in over a decade, it really hit me how every day is a rerun. Do you know what I mean? They never do a new episode-- every day, the same words, same songs, in the same order, stretching back hundreds of years. They read a new part of the Bible, part of the Torah, some days, so there's that. But all the rest-- basically exactly the same every day.
<v Ira Glass>我小时候一直喜欢去犹太教堂。我们经常去。所以回去感觉不错。我能背诵所有的希伯来语祈祷文。我不知道这好不好，但由于十多年没去过犹太教堂，我真的意识到每一天都是重播。你明白我的意思吗？他们从不演新剧集——每一天，同样的词，同样的歌，同样的顺序，延续了数百年。他们有时会读圣经的一新部分，托拉的一部分，所以还有那一项。但其余的——基本上每天都完全一样。

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:48.830
<v Ira Glass>And everybody is singing and chanting. And I started looking in the prayer book on the side of the page with the English translation of the prayers, which I hadn't done in years. And I really was struck at how many of them-- the Amidah, the Ashrei, are about praising God at length. That's what the words mean, even the Kaddish, which you say over and over during services.
<v Ira Glass>每个人都在歌唱和吟诵。我开始看祈祷书侧面页上的英语翻译，这我已经多年没做过了。我真的很震惊于其中有多少内容——如阿米达，阿什雷——都是关于长时间赞美神的。这就是这些词的意思，甚至是你在仪式中不断诵读的卡迪什。

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:53.570
<v Ira Glass>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Ira Glass>[讲希伯来语]

00:02:53.570 --> 00:03:23.500
<v Ira Glass>It goes on and on. And the words basically are, may His great name be exalted and sanctified, blessed and praised, glorified and exalted, extolled and honored, adored and lauded, be the name of the Holy One. And that is what they have say when your mom dies-- comforting, huh? It's basically God is great, over and over, building to this beautiful line, really beautiful, that's basically: God is so great, it's beyond the power of any prayer or word or song or praise. It's beyond the power of language to capture it.
<v Ira Glass>它没完没了。这些词的意思基本上是，愿祂伟大的名字被尊崇和圣化，被祝福和赞美，被荣耀和升华，被颂扬和尊荣，被崇拜和称颂，愿圣者的名字被赞美。这就是当你母亲去世时他们让你说的话——很安慰人，是吧？基本上就是神很伟大，一遍又一遍，最终引向这一行美丽的句子，真的很美，基本上就是：神如此伟大，超越了任何祈祷、言语、歌曲或赞美的力量。它超越了语言所能捕捉的范畴。

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:44.570
<v Ira Glass>And it really hit me, sitting there. What does God get out of that? Why does he want us sitting down and telling him how great he is for 45 minutes a day? Is he that needy? If some parent demanded that of their kids-- OK, I want you to praise me for 45 minutes a day, every single day of your life, we would know they were nuts.
<v Ira Glass>坐在那里，我真的深受触动。神从那里面得到了什么？他为什么要我们每天坐45分钟来告诉他他有多伟大？他那么需要吗？如果有些父母那样要求他们的孩子——好的，我想要你们每天、余生每一天都赞美我45分钟，我们就会知道他们疯了。

00:03:44.570 --> 00:04:15.300
<v Ira Glass>And it's like what I was saying to John-- what does God care if we love him? And John had such a lovely answer. He said, first off, he thinks lots of people make the mistake of picturing God as being like us, like humans, like he's somebody who we're calling on the phone, or something, when we're praying. But the way he sees it, he understands God to be all the values and principles that he sees in scripture-- the obligation to love each other, to be honest and decent in our dealings with each other, all of those things.
<v Ira Glass>就像我跟约翰说的——如果我们要爱他，神会在乎吗？约翰给出了一个非常可爱的回答。他说，首先，他认为很多人犯了一个错误，把神想象成我们人类，就像我们打电话时联系的某个人。但在他看来，他将神理解为他在经文中看到的所有价值观和原则——爱彼此、在相互交往中诚实正直的义务，所有这些事情。

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:34.640
<v Ira Glass>And when he's praising God, he said, that's what he's praising. He's basically re-pledging himself to those principles, which he loves. In other words, the literal words of the Bible, the literal words of the prayers, aren't as important as that pledge, a pledge to act a certain way in the world.
<v Ira Glass>他说，当他在赞美神时，那就是他所赞美的。他基本上是在重新承诺遵循这些原则，他热爱这些原则。换句话说，圣经的字面词语，祈祷文的字面词语，并不像那个承诺那样重要，那个承诺是在世上以某种方式行事的承诺。

00:04:34.640 --> 00:05:03.470
<v Ira Glass>And reading and calling around in the days since we had that conversation, I've learned that other clergy-- Christian, Jewish, Muslim-- some of them say the same things, especially that God doesn't need our prayers. The prayers are for us. One of them told me the Kaddish is supposed to comfort me after my mom's death by pointing me to this idea of God's presence in the world, the goodness in the world, which, if I believed in God, I guess could be a comfort.
<v Ira Glass>自从我们谈话后的这些天里，通过阅读和四处询问，我了解到其他神职人员——基督教、犹太教、穆斯林——有些人也这么说，特别是神不需要我们的祈祷。祈祷是为了我们自己。其中一人告诉我，卡迪什旨在通过向我指出神在世上的存在、世上的善良，来在我母亲去世后安慰我，如果我相信神，我想这可能是一种安慰。

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:34.580
<v Ira Glass>But I don't believe. But weirdly, even without that, without believing any of the words, I do find it's a comfort to say the prayer. It's just it's familiar. It's familiar as the nursery rhymes my mom sang to me as a kid, as the Shema, the prayer that she had me and my sister say every night before we went to sleep. It's comforting, despite the fact that it's in another language and part of a doctrine I don't believe anymore.
<v Ira Glass>但我并不相信。但奇怪的是，即使没有那种信仰，即使不相信任何字句，我也觉得诵读这篇祈祷文是一种安慰。它只是很熟悉。它像我母亲小时候唱给我的儿歌一样熟悉，像“示马”一样熟悉，那是她让我和姐姐每晚睡前诵读的祈祷文。尽管它是用另一种语言，且属于我不再相信的教义，但它依然令人安慰。

00:05:34.580 --> 00:06:00.650
<v Ira Glass>Just the fact of it handed to me by my parents and to them by their parents-- Freida and Lou and Melvin and Molly; and their parents before them-- David, Elizabeth, and Isidore; and people whose names I don't even know; and before them, their parents for hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of years; and standing in synagogue that day, standing and saying these words in unison with other mourners, it was comforting.
<v Ira Glass>仅仅是它由父母传给我，由他们的父母传给他们——弗丽达、卢、梅尔文和莫莉；以及他们之前的父母——大卫、伊丽莎白和伊西多尔；还有我甚至不知道名字的人；以及他们之前几百几百几百年的父母；那天站在犹太教堂里，和其他哀悼者一起站着诵读这些词句，这令人安慰。

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:15.430
<v Ira Glass>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Ira Glass>[讲希伯来语]

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:45.610
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, the sometimes very old words that we turn to for comfort in moments of adversity and at other times, they can be very random-- the novel Little Women, A Tribe Called Quest and other old school hip hop-- we have stories about each of those. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们有时会求助于那些非常古老的词句以获得安慰，而在其他时候，它们可能非常随机——小说《小妇人》、探索部落组合和其他老派嘻哈——我们有关于每一个的故事。来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:06:45.610 --> 00:07:02.920
<v Ira Glass>Act 1-- Go to the Mattresses. Shamyla always loved books. Back in 1989, like lots of other 11-year-olds, she also loved Punky Brewster and Paula Abdul. But her main thing was reading-- The Baby-Sitters Club books, Little House on the Prairie. It was all about words and books.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕——去干活。沙米拉一直很爱书。回到1989年，像许多其他11岁的孩子一样，她也喜欢庞基·布鲁斯特和宝拉·阿巴杜。但她的主要兴趣是阅读——《保姆俱乐部》系列书，《草原小屋》。一切都与文字和书有关。

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:17.240
<v Ira Glass>And then something happened, which totally changed what she read and how. Elna Baker talked to her. This is a story we first broadcast a few years ago. Today's show is a rerun. Here's Elna.
<v Ira Glass>然后发生了一件事，彻底改变了她读什么以及如何读。埃尔娜·贝克采访了她。这是我们几年前首播的一个故事。今天的节目是重播。这是埃尔娜。

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:22.610
<v Elna Baker>OK, so you meet somebody at a party, and they ask you, what's your favorite book? What's your answer?
<v Elna Baker>好的，如果你在派对上遇到某人，他们问你，你最喜欢的书是什么？你的答案是什么？

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.640
<v Shamyla>My answer is Little Women is my all-time favorite book.
<v Shamyla>我的答案是《小妇人》是我一直以来最喜欢的书。

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:29.570
<v Elna Baker>And then if they were to ask you, why? What would you say?
<v Elna Baker>如果他们问你，为什么？你会怎么说？

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:51.050
<v Shamyla>Well, I probably would have a very long conversation with someone about that. But it goes back to the '90s, when I was living in Pakistan. And this was the only book that I had in my possession for a couple of years.
<v Shamyla>嗯，我可能会和某人就此进行很长的交谈。但这要追溯到90年代，当时我住在巴基斯坦。这是我几年来拥有的唯一一本书。

00:07:51.050 --> 00:08:03.670
<v Elna Baker>A couple of years, when she was basically held in captivity in Pakistan. I'll tell you how she found herself holed up with only one book in a minute. But first, here's how she ended up in captivity.
<v Elna Baker>几年，当她基本上被扣押在巴基斯坦时。稍后我会告诉你她是如何发现自己只带着一本书被困在那里的。但首先，这是她最终被俘的过程。

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:19.020
<v Elna Baker>She was raised in suburban Maryland. Her father was an economist, her mom a teacher. She went to an all-girls prep school. At 11, she learned her parents weren't her biological parents. Her mom, the oldest daughter in her family, couldn't have kids and really wanted to.
<v Elna Baker>她在马里兰州郊区长大。她父亲是经济学家，母亲是老师。她上了一所全女子预科学校。11岁时，她得知父母不是她的亲生父母。她的母亲，家里最大的女儿，不能生育，但非常想要孩子。

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:36.690
<v Elna Baker>And so Shamyla's family came up with a solution, one that's not unusual in Pakistan. Since her mom was the oldest, it was her younger sister's obligation to give her a child. And because of this family pressure and a sense of duty, the younger sister did it. But then it got complicated.
<v Elna Baker>所以沙米拉的家庭想出了一个解决方案，这在巴基斯坦并不罕见。既然她母亲是最大的，那她妹妹有义务送给她一个孩子。由于这种家庭压力和责任感，妹妹照做了。但随后变得复杂起来。

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:53.500
<v Shamyla>Well, my adoptive parents had kids. They had a son. And then they had another son. And that really made my biological family confused and angry. And they were like, we gave you our child, and now you have your own children. Give us our daughter back.
<v Shamyla>嗯，我的养父母有了孩子。他们有一个儿子。然后他们又有了另一个儿子。这真的让我的亲生家庭感到困惑和愤怒。他们说，我们给了你我们的孩子，现在你有你自己的孩子了。把我们的女儿还给我们。

00:08:53.500 --> 00:09:00.930
<v Shamyla>And my mom said, I'm not going to give her back. She's not a ball. I can't toss her back.
<v Shamyla>我母亲说，我不会还给她的。她不是一个球。我不能把她扔回去。

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:18.750
<v Elna Baker>For years, they fought over this. When Shamyla was 12, on a visit home to Pakistan, her birth parents asked if they could take her on a trip. Her mom agreed, as a gesture of goodwill. It'd just be a week long, and she'd get to know her cousins, who were actually her brothers. And at first, it was really fun.
<v Elna Baker>多年来，他们为此争吵。沙米拉12岁时，在一次回巴基斯坦的探亲中，她的亲生父母问能否带她去旅行。为了表示善意，她母亲同意了。那只会持续一周，她可以认识一下其实是她兄弟的表亲们。起初，这真的很有趣。

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:34.540
<v Shamyla>Until I got really sick at someone's house. And it turned out I had malaria. And what I didn't realize at the time was that they had taken me there on purpose, because it was way out in the country, where no one could find me.
<v Shamyla>直到我在某人家里病得很重。结果我得了疟疾。当时我没意识到的是，他们是故意把我带到那里的，因为那是偏远农村，没人能找到我。

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:39.820
<v Elna Baker>Two weeks go by, then three. She's all better, asking when she can go back.
<v Elna Baker>两周过去了，然后是三周。她痊愈了，问什么时候可以回去。

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:56.860
<v Shamyla>And they kept making excuses-- um, not-- you just need to wait. We have some other places to go. And I remember, at some point, my biological father taking me aside very kindly and saying, you know, we're your real family. They never loved you. We love you.
<v Shamyla>他们一直找借口——嗯，不要——你只需要等等。我们还有其他地方要去。我记得在某个时刻，我的亲生父亲非常友善地把我拉到一边说，你知道，我们才是你的真正家人。他们从来没爱过你。我们爱你。

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:10.900
<v Shamyla>They loved you like the way people love slippers, like an old, used pair of slippers. But they love their boys, because that's their blood. And we're your blood, and that's how we love you. So you're staying with us now.
<v Shamyla>他们爱你就像人们爱拖鞋那样，就像一双旧的、用过的拖鞋。但他们爱他们的男孩，因为那是他们的血脉。而我们是你的血脉，这就是我们爱你的方式。所以你现在和我们待在一起。

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:22.570
<v Elna Baker>They don't let her leave, and she's really confused-- of course, my US parents love me. They must love me. But then, if they loved me, why aren't they coming to get me?
<v Elna Baker>他们不让她离开，她真的感到困惑——当然，我的美国父母爱我。他们一定爱我。但如果他们爱我，为什么他们不来接我？

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:42.610
<v Elna Baker>Unbeknownst to her, her US parents are trying to find out where she is. But no one will tell them. To make matters worse, her US parents are not legally Shamyla's parents. The adoption was never formal. She was just handed over. So they have no legal claim to their daughter, and there's nothing Shamyla could do. She was 12.
<v Elna Baker>她不知道的是，她的美国父母正在试图查明她在哪里。但没人告诉他们。更糟糕的是，她的美国父母在法律上并不是沙米拉的父母。领养从未正式化。她只是被交给了他们。所以他们对女儿没有法律主张，沙米拉也无能为力。她才12岁。

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:53.670
<v Shamyla>Even if I fantasized about running away to another city and seeing other relatives, I was like, how will I do it? What will I do? Where will I get the money? How will I get on the train? How will I get on a plane? What will I do?
<v Shamyla>即使我幻想过逃到另一个城市去看望其他亲戚，我也想，我该怎么做？我会做什么？我从哪弄钱？我怎么坐火车？我怎么坐飞机？我该怎么办？

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:54.640
<v Elna Baker>You were trapped.
<v Elna Baker>你被困住了。

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.670
<v Shamyla>I was trapped, yeah.
<v Shamyla>我被困住了，是的。

00:10:56.670 --> 00:11:25.610
<v Elna Baker>She says her birth parents told her, when she was growing up, whenever they saw her, it was upsetting to them. They thought, she's too Americanized. They were incredibly traditional, even for Pakistan, which is already very patriarchal. Women are expected to be subordinate. And if their purity is questioned, like if they're even seen speaking to a man in a public place, their family's reputation could be ruined, and the whole family could be ostracized from the community for it.
<v Elna Baker>她说她的亲生父母告诉她，当她长大时，只要看到她，他们就很心烦。他们认为，她太美国化了。即便对于巴基斯坦这样一个已经极其重男轻女的国家来说，他们也非常传统。女性被期望处于从属地位。如果她们的贞洁受到质疑，比如如果有人看到她们在公共场合与男性交谈，她们家庭的名声可能会被毁掉，整个家庭都可能因此被社区驱逐。

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:48.670
<v Elna Baker>This is especially the case in Peshawar, where her birth parents lived. And Shamyla, a suburban Maryland tween, didn't understand that this was the world she lived in now. She says everything about the way she acted was wrong in their eyes. They didn't approve of the music she listened to or the books she read. They thought they were teaching her to be far too independent for a girl.
<v Elna Baker>这在白沙瓦（她亲生父母居住的地方）尤其如此。而沙米拉，一个马里兰郊区的青春期少女，并不明白这就是她现在生活的世界。她说，在他们眼里，她的一切行为方式都是错的。他们不赞成她听的音乐或读的书。他们认为他们在教导她，对于一个女孩来说太独立了。

00:11:48.670 --> 00:12:06.860
<v Elna Baker>At this rate, she'd become promiscuous any day now. And since they didn't trust her not to be, they kept her under house arrest, confining her to a corner of the house that consisted of a bedroom and a bathroom. And they confiscated her cassettes and her books. Her birth father would lecture to her for hours.
<v Elna Baker>照这个速度，她很快就会变得滥交。由于他们不相信她不会那样，他们把她软禁起来，把她限制在房子里由卧室和浴室组成的角落里。他们没收了她的磁带和书籍。她的亲生父亲会给她讲几个小时的课。

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:30.220
<v Shamyla>His favorite three topics were America is a bad place, especially for girls. American women are loose character and horrible. The second topic was your adopted family never really loved you, never. And the third topic was, so here's what you're going to do when you get married. These are your wifely duties, including your sexual obligations to your husband.
<v Shamyla>他最喜欢的三个话题是：美国是一个糟糕的地方，尤其是对女孩来说。美国女性品行不端，非常可怕。第二个话题是你的养父母从来没有真正爱过你，从来没有。第三个话题是，当你结婚时你该怎么做。这是你的妻子职责，包括你对丈夫的性义务。

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:53.870
<v Elna Baker>He'd also lecture her about the rules she needed to follow. Some were the normal rules of an observant Muslim home. She couldn't go out without being covered, make eye contact, wear her hair down. Others were cultural-- she couldn't hug people; wear glasses when guests came over; speak English or Urdu, which would be seen as a sign of modernity. Only Pashto, the language of the region, was allowed.
<v Elna Baker>他还会给她讲她需要遵守的规则。有些是虔诚穆斯林家庭的正常规则。她不能在不遮盖身体的情况下外出，不能有目光接触，不能披散头发。其他的则是文化性的——她不能拥抱别人；客人来时不能戴眼镜；不能说英语或乌尔都语，这被视为现代化的标志。只允许说普什图语，即该地区的语言。

00:12:53.870 --> 00:13:11.920
<v Elna Baker>She couldn't serve herself food before her brothers. She was barely allowed to eat. They thought she needed to be thin to get married, so they locked the fridge. She says, if she messed up, she was beaten with a kitchen squeegee, which cut her face open, or a walking stick. That was the worst one, she says.
<v Elna Baker>她不能在兄弟之前给自己端食物。她几乎不被允许吃饭。他们认为她需要瘦一点才能结婚，所以他们锁上了冰箱。她说，如果她搞砸了，她会被厨房刮水板殴打，那把她的脸割开了，或者被拐杖殴打。她说那是最糟糕的一次。

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:19.900
<v Shamyla>The second worst one was with a golf club. And the third worst one was with cleats near my eye.
<v Shamyla>第二糟糕的是用高尔夫球杆。第三糟糕的是被高跟鞋踢到眼睛附近。

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:21.350
<v Elna Baker>They took cleats and hit you with them.
<v Elna Baker>他们用高跟鞋踢你。

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.950
<v Shamyla>And I think I got kicked.
<v Shamyla>我想我被踢了。

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:42.110
<v Elna Baker>She asked me not to reach out to her birth parents to confirm these details, because she was worried for her safety. And I didn't. But members of her family here in the States confirmed that this is what her birth parents are like. And her mother and therapist corroborated that her story of being abused has always remained consistent.
<v Elna Baker>她要求我不要联系她的亲生父母来证实这些细节，因为她担心自己的安全。我没那样做。但她在国美的家人成员证实了这就是她亲生父母的样子。她的母亲和治疗师证实，她关于被虐待的说法一直保持一致。

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:54.970
<v Shamyla>I remember, after every time they would beat me, I'd put an X on whatever calendar I had and be like, this will never happen again. I will be so good now, that this will never happen again.
<v Shamyla>我记得，每次他们打我之后，我都会在日历上打个叉，心想，这再也不会发生了。我现在会表现得非常好，这样这再也不会发生了。

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:58.000
<v Elna Baker>Like, from this day forward, I'll finally be a good girl.
<v Elna Baker>就像，从今天起，我终于会成为一个好女孩。

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:04.590
<v Shamyla>Yep.
<v Shamyla>是的。

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:08.040
<v Elna Baker>Did you feel like you'd never be the girl they wanted you to be?
<v Elna Baker>你觉得你永远无法成为他们想要的那个女孩吗？

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:14.980
<v Shamyla>No, I thought eventually, I'm going to get there. I just have to keep trying. I really thought that eventually, I'm going to become that person.
<v Shamyla>不，我想最终，我会做到的。我只需要继续努力。我真的以为最终我会变成那个人。

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:17.170
<v Elna Baker>And you wanted to be that person?
<v Elna Baker>你想要成为那个人吗？

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.550
<v Shamyla>I think that I thought I had no choice.
<v Shamyla>我想我以为我别无选择。

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:34.050
<v Elna Baker>She wasn't just physically abused. As she got older, she says, she was also sexually abused by her brother. And finally, into this bleakness, comes a book, the book, Little Women.
<v Elna Baker>她不仅受到身体虐待。随着年龄增长，她说，她还被兄弟性虐待。最后，在这片阴郁中，一本书出现了，那就是《小妇人》。

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:59.680
<v Shamyla>So one of my friends noticed the book at a book fair at our school. All these books had come from Singapore. And there was Little Women. I said, I know this book, and I want it. And I remember thinking, oh my god, I read that book when I was little. I had a huge, beautiful illustrated copy growing up. And I said to my friend Mirjana, I was like, Mirjana, can you please buy it? It's 10 rupees, and I'll pay you back.
<v Shamyla>我的一位朋友在学校的书展上注意到了这本书。所有这些书都来自新加坡。然后就是《小妇人》。我说，我知道这本书，我想要它。我记得我当时想，天哪，我小时候读过那本书。我成长过程中有一本巨大、精美的插图版。我对我朋友米里亚娜说，米里亚娜，你能帮我买下它吗？只要10卢比，我会还给你的。

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:13.480
<v Elna Baker>She'd tried smuggling books home before, but she'd always been caught. She hid the book inside her mattress pad underneath the bed. And she broke it into sections, roughly eight piles, so that it didn't make the mattress stick up in any way.
<v Elna Baker>她以前试过偷运书回家，但总是被抓。她把书藏在床下的床垫垫子里。她把书拆成若干部分，大约八堆，这样就不会使床垫鼓起来。

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:23.500
<v Elna Baker>When she heard her family go out, she'd wait, and then carefully unzip the mattress pad, and take a section of the book out at a time. Whichever one she pulled out first, she read.
<v Elna Baker>当她听到家人出去时，她会等待，然后小心翼翼地拉开床垫垫子，一次取出一部分。她先拿出哪一部分，就读哪一部分。

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:37.240
<v Shamyla>It was the book of my life. It was the only book I had to escape. It was the only book that I had to actually read over and over again. And I kind of memorized it.
<v Shamyla>这是我生命之书。这是我唯一能逃离的书。这是我唯一能一遍又一遍阅读的书。我基本上把它背下来了。

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:39.700
<v Elna Baker>I'm going to read you like some quotes from the book.
<v Elna Baker>我要给你读一些书里的引语。

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:40.270
<v Shamyla>OK.
<v Shamyla>好的。

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:47.110
<v Elna Baker>And just interrupt if you know where it is or what this is about. And then I'm going to just random open book.
<v Elna Baker>如果你知道它在哪里或者是关于什么的，就打断我。然后我只是随机打开书。

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:50.010
<v Shamyla>[CHUCKLING] This feels like a pop quiz.
<v Shamyla>[轻笑]这感觉像个突击测验。

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:57.290
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, yeah. Ooh: Mercy on me. Beth loves Laurie, she said, sitting down in--
<v Elna Baker>是的，是的。哦：可怜的我。贝丝爱着劳里，她说，坐在——

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:05.570
<v Shamyla>Oh, this is when Beth is ill. And Beth is changing, and she thinks the secret is that Beth is in love with Laurie.
<v Shamyla>哦，这是贝丝生病的时候。贝丝正在发生变化，她认为秘密是贝丝爱上了劳里。

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:11.890
<v Elna Baker>Mrs. March and Jo were deep in their own affairs, when a sound from Meg made them look up to see her staring at her note.
<v Elna Baker>马奇太太和乔正忙于自己的事情，这时梅格发出的声音让她们抬起头，看到她正盯着她的便条。

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:22.610
<v Shamyla>Oh, that's when-- [LAUGHING] that's when Meg has written to Mr. Brooke, and he actually writes back. But it was Laurie pretending to be Mr. Brooke.
<v Shamyla>哦，那是——[大笑]那是梅格写信给布鲁克先生的时候，他真的回信了。但那是劳里假装是布鲁克先生。

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:26.930
<v Elna Baker>We did this eight times, and she's able to get everything I read within seconds.
<v Elna Baker>我们这样做了八次，我读的每一段她都能在几秒钟内接上。

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:27.950
<v Elna Baker>You're good at this.
<v Elna Baker>你在这方面很在行。

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:32.780
<v Shamyla>[LAUGHING] I told you, I read this book over and over and over.
<v Shamyla>[大笑]我告诉过你，我一遍又一遍、反反复复地读这本书。

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:34.220
<v Elna Baker>How many times?
<v Elna Baker>读了多少遍？

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:46.160
<v Shamyla>Oh, hundreds. By now, it's probably thousands.
<v Shamyla>哦，几百遍。到现在，可能已经几千遍了。

00:16:46.160 --> 00:17:09.320
<v Elna Baker>If you haven't read Little Women, it's basically about four sisters, the March sisters. Meg, the oldest, is the responsible one. Jo is the rebel and the writer. Beth is sick. And Amy, the youngest, is a spoiled brat. Their father is serving in the Civil War, and their mother is raising them alone. The book follows these girls from childhood into adulthood.
<v Elna Baker>如果你没读过《小妇人》，它基本讲的是四个姐妹，马奇姐妹。梅格，老大，是负责任的那个。乔是叛逆者和作家。贝丝生病了。艾米，最小的，是个被宠坏的调皮鬼。她们的父亲在参加内战，母亲独自抚养她们。这本书讲述了这些女孩从童年到成年的过程。

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:13.070
<v Elna Baker>Were there parts of the book that you would just read over and over, like, your favorite parts?
<v Elna Baker>书里有没有你反反复复读的部分，比如，你最喜欢的部分？

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:27.920
<v Shamyla>I loved reading the parts in the beginning, when the two sisters would talk to each other, Meg and Jo, and their bond. Because I didn't have a sister, and I had no one to talk to. And I really loved the scenes about parties and stuff.
<v Shamyla>我喜欢读开始的部分，两个姐妹梅格和乔互相交谈的时候，以及她们之间的联系。因为我没有姐妹，没人跟我说话。我真的很喜欢关于派对之类的场景。

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:44.600
<v Elna Baker>She loved these scenes, because they mirrored her life in a really specific way. Her birth parents were upper middle class, and they wanted to use Shamyla to marry into a higher class. So they dressed her up and took her to social events to show her off. In the book, there are events like this that the March sisters go to.
<v Elna Baker>她喜欢这些场景，因为它们以一种非常具体的方式映射了她的生活。她的亲生父母是中上阶层，他们想利用沙米拉嫁入更高阶层。所以他们给她打扮，带她去社交活动炫耀她。书里也有马奇姐妹参加的类似活动。

00:17:44.600 --> 00:18:03.500
<v Shamyla>There's a chapter about Meg and Jo going to a party in the very beginning. And they're not out yet in society. And I was like, Oh my god, this 1860-something book is what's happening in Peshawar in 1990. Oh my god, it's the same thing. I'm not out yet in society, and I have to be very careful how I present myself.
<v Shamyla>开始有一章是关于梅格和乔去参加派对的。她们在社交界还未出道。我就想，哦，天哪，这本1860年左右的书里发生的事情，正在1990年的白沙瓦上演。哦，天哪，一模一样。我还没出道，我必须非常小心地展现自己。

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:18.980
<v Elna Baker>In a way, Little Women was the perfect book for Shamyla's situation, because it's like a how-to for girls, a survival guide. Like most women who read the book, the sister Shamyla relates to the most is Jo. She was my favorite, too.
<v Elna Baker>在某种程度上，《小妇人》是沙米拉处境的完美之书，因为它就像女孩的操作指南，生存指南。像大多数读这本书的女性一样，沙米拉最认同的姐妹是乔。她也是我最喜欢的。

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:39.080
<v Elna Baker>I was raised in a strict religion, and my life was supposed to be about marriage and motherhood. And then Jo came into my orbit and showed me I could have ambition, and be improper, and break the rules. And I loved her for it. And that's how Shamyla felt. Jo helped Shamyla hold on to the identity her family was trying so hard to squash.
<v Elna Baker>我在一个严格的宗教环境中长大，我的生活本应关于婚姻和母亲身份。然后乔进入了我的视野，向我展示我可以有野心，可以不循规蹈矩，可以打破规则。我为此爱她。沙米拉也是这样感受的。乔帮助沙米拉守住了她的家庭极力想压制的身份认同。

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:57.660
<v Shamyla>There's a part where Jo and Laurie go running. And Meg scolds her and says, when will you learn to be ladylike? And she says, I hope never. I always want to run, and I always want to romp. And I thought, me too. I never want to have to stop being spirited and independent.
<v Shamyla>有一部分是乔和劳里在奔跑。梅格责备她说，你什么时候才能学会像个淑女？她说，我希望永远不会。我一直想奔跑，一直想嬉戏。我想，我也是。我永远不想停止做一个充满活力和独立的人。

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:13.680
<v Shamyla>And I remember Jo had a temper in the book. And I thought, I have a temper, too. And she's working really hard to curb it, and I was like, I am, too. I didn't have any of the freedom Jo had. But in my heart, I was just as feisty and independent.
<v Shamyla>我记得书里的乔脾气暴躁。我想，我的脾气也暴躁。她正非常努力地克制它，我想，我也一样。我没有乔那样的自由。但在心里，我和她一样充满活力且独立。

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:24.240
<v Shamyla>So I think it's very funny when nowadays, my friends are like, well, you were so meek. And I was like, I was never meek. That's just a facade. [LAUGHING] I'm Jo. I get her.
<v Shamyla>所以我认为很有趣的是，现在朋友们说，好吧，你太温顺了。我就说，我从来都不温顺。那只是个伪装。[大笑]我是乔。我懂她。

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:33.200
<v Elna Baker>But in a way, what's so interesting is it's so literally like your way of being Jo was reading about Jo. That's where you actually got to be Jo.
<v Elna Baker>但有趣的是，你成为乔的方式如此字面地就是通过阅读关于乔的内容。那就是你真正能成为乔的地方。

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:41.810
<v Shamyla>Yeah, I couldn't really do anything else.
<v Shamyla>是的，我真的没法做别的。

00:19:41.810 --> 00:20:06.590
<v Elna Baker>In the book, Jo writes stories. Shamyla loved to write, too. But she says, the second year she was there, her birth father declared, girls aren't supposed to write, and took a notebook filled with her stories, and burned it in the backyard, and made her watch. In the book, Jo writes her stories alone in the attic. This gave Shamyla the idea to sneak into her bathroom with a fountain pen and paper and write.
<v Elna Baker>在书里，乔写故事。沙米拉也爱写故事。但她说，她在那里第二年时，亲生父亲宣布，女孩不应该写作，没收了一本写满她故事的笔记本，并在后院烧了它，还让她看着。书里，乔独自在阁楼写她的故事。这给了沙米拉想法，偷偷溜进浴室，拿着钢笔和纸写。

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:12.920
<v Shamyla>And then I would wash all the ink off the paper, crumple up the wet paper, and throw it up the hole in the window mesh.
<v Shamyla>然后我会把纸上的墨水全部洗掉，揉皱湿纸，把它们扔出窗户网眼里的孔。

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:18.320
<v Elna Baker>Wait, you would write the stories and then erase everything you'd written?
<v Elna Baker>等等，你会写故事然后把你写的一切都擦掉？

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:28.250
<v Shamyla>So there was a hose that attached from the tub to the sink. And so I would take that hose and wash all the ink off.
<v Shamyla>有一根软管连接着浴缸和水槽。我会拿着软管把所有的墨水洗掉。

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:29.630
<v Elna Baker>Down the drain of--
<v Elna Baker>顺着下水道——

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:39.080
<v Shamyla>There was a floor drain, and I'd make sure that the floor drain was not blue with the blue ink [LAUGHING] and that there was no sign of ink that had fallen off the papers.
<v Shamyla>有一个地漏，我会确保地漏没有被蓝色墨水弄蓝，[大笑]并且没有任何墨水从纸上掉落的痕迹。

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:57.080
<v Elna Baker>Shamyla knew how our family would react if they caught her writing or if they found her copy of Little Women and read any of it. Like, there's a boy in the book. His name is Laurie. He and Jo have an innocent flirtation. But Shamyla says, if her birth parents read that part, they'd think--
<v Elna Baker>沙米拉知道如果他们发现她在写作，或者发现她那本《小妇人》并读到其中的任何内容，她的家人会如何反应。比如，书里有个男孩。他叫劳里。他和乔有一段天真的调情。但沙米拉说，如果她的亲生父母读到那部分，他们会认为——

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:10.220
<v Shamyla>You don't go over to adolescent boy's house ever. There's no innocence in this. In real life, Jo and Laurie would be doing very bad things. This is not right.
<v Shamyla>你永远不要去青春期男孩家里。这根本不天真。在现实生活中，乔和劳里会做非常坏的事情。这不对。

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:13.940
<v Elna Baker>Well, they're saying, in real life, Jo and Laurie would be getting it on?
<v Elna Baker>嗯，他们是在说，在现实生活中，乔和劳里会搞在一起？

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:16.540
<v Shamyla>Basically.
<v Shamyla>基本上。

00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:18.310
<v Elna Baker>I mean, that would have been a better book, right?
<v Elna Baker>我的意思是，那会是本更好的书，对吧？

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:19.600
<v Shamyla>[LAUGHING]
<v Shamyla>[大笑]

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:26.470
<v Elna Baker>I loved that. Oh my gosh I was like, in my fan fiction, I would write that.
<v Elna Baker>我喜欢那个。哦天哪，我当时想，在我的同人小说里，我会写那个。

00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:36.830
<v Shamyla>That's so funny.
<v Shamyla>那太搞笑了。

00:21:36.830 --> 00:22:07.820
<v Elna Baker>After a year, Shamyla's American mother was allowed to visit her. Shamyla told her what was happening. Her American mother cried and begged for her extended family to intervene and send Shamyla back to the States. But it was useless. Shamyla felt abandoned and hopeless. So she gave in, did her best to make herself into the obedient Pashtun girl they wanted her to be. There's a scene in the book, one I hated, because it was so sad, that Shamyla read over and over again.
<v Elna Baker>一年后，沙米拉的美国母亲获准探望她。沙米拉告诉了她发生了什么。她的美国母亲哭了，恳求她的大家族干预并将沙米拉送回美国。但那是徒劳的。沙米拉感到被遗弃和绝望。所以她屈服了，尽最大努力让自己成为他们想要的顺从的普什图女孩。书里有一个场景，我讨厌它，因为它太悲伤了，沙米拉一遍又一遍地阅读。

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:34.020
<v Shamyla>I reread Beth's dying a lot, because there was this sense of helplessness. And I really understood that in a way that a lot of people don't. Because I knew, when something's inevitable, and it's going to happen, they keep saying, the tide is going out, in the book. And I was like, yeah, when the tide goes out, you just have to go with it. You can't fight it. I understood that on a level I couldn't even explain. I just knew what that meant.
<v Shamyla>我重读了贝丝去世的部分，因为有一种无助感。我以一种很多人不理解的方式真正理解了这一点。因为我知道，当某事不可避免，必然会发生时，他们在书里一直说，潮水正在退去。我就想，是的，当潮水退去时，你只能随波逐流。你无法抗争。我在一个我甚至无法解释的层面上理解这一点。我只是知道那意味着什么。

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:36.660
<v Elna Baker>It sounds like their world feels so real to you.
<v Elna Baker>听起来他们的世界对你来说感觉如此真实。

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:41.240
<v Shamyla>[LAUGHING] I know. It really does. I felt like I lived with them sometimes.
<v Shamyla>[大笑]我知道。真的如此。我觉得自己有时和他们住在一起。

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:42.200
<v Elna Baker>What do you mean?
<v Elna Baker>你是什么意思？

00:22:42.200 --> 00:23:03.070
<v Shamyla>I felt like I was watching them, like I was the servant, or something, watching. I felt like I lived in the attic, and I could go downstairs and see them. They couldn't see me, but I could see them. And I would join the world. I would join in.
<v Shamyla>我觉得我在看着他们，就像我是仆人，或者什么的，在看。我觉得我住在阁楼里，我可以下楼去看他们。他们看不见我，但我看得见他们。我会加入这个世界。我会加入其中。

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:14.780
<v Elna Baker>Her real world, of course was much darker. By the time she got to 17 or 18, she concluded that there's really only one way out to escape her family and maybe even have a happy life.
<v Elna Baker>当然，她的真实世界要黑暗得多。到她17或18岁时，她断定逃离家人并可能拥有幸福生活只有一条路。

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:30.070
<v Shamyla>I was like, OK, so the way out is marriage. So I have to play this game to get married. And I started to actually kind of be like, OK, so in Little Women, you know how Meg goes to society dances, and wears her glove, and starts putting her hair up?
<v Shamyla>我想，好吧，那么出路就是婚姻。所以我必须玩这个游戏来结婚。我实际上开始想，好吧，在《小妇人》里，你知道梅格如何去参加社交舞会，戴上手套，开始把头发梳上去吗？

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:47.740
<v Shamyla>I was like, I can do this. I can do this to get the best possible way out. I was really thinking about how to create my life with someone else and what that would look like. And so I had stopped so much caring about going home or going back.
<v Shamyla>我想，我可以做到。我可以这样做来得到最好的出路。我真的在考虑如何与另一个人创造我的生活，那会是什么样子。所以我已经不再那么关心回家或回去的事情了。

00:23:47.740 --> 00:24:00.400
<v Elna Baker>And marriage is actually what the second part of Little Women is about. The second half of the book is very different from the first. It covers domestic duties and how the girls let go of their wild ways to become good wives.
<v Elna Baker>而婚姻实际上是《小妇人》第二部分的内容。书的后半部分和前半部分非常不同。它涵盖了家庭责任，以及女孩们如何放下狂野的本性成为好妻子。

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:03.250
<v Elna Baker>Shamyla, not a big fan of Part Two.
<v Elna Baker>沙米拉不太喜欢第二部分。

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:09.100
<v Shamyla>The book, the second book, I remember thinking, I don't like this part. I like going back to their childhood and reading that part again.
<v Shamyla>这本书，第二本书，我记得当时想，我不喜欢这部分。我喜欢回到她们的童年，再读那部分。

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:11.320
<v Elna Baker>Oh, the second part is the worst.
<v Elna Baker>哦，第二部分是最糟糕的。

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:14.540
<v Shamyla>I know. It's so, so, so sad.
<v Shamyla>我知道。太、太、太悲伤了。

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:31.280
<v Elna Baker>I think I'd really forgotten the message of the second half of the book. And it's kind of infuriating, when you realize how, in some ways, it feels almost like a propaganda for becoming the way you're supposed to be.
<v Elna Baker>我想我真的忘记了书后半部分的信息。当你意识到在某些方面，它感觉几乎像是让你成为你“应该”成为的那种人的宣传品时，这有点让人愤怒。

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:56.930
<v Shamyla>Yeah. I remember thinking, when Meg gets married, they have a whole chapter about how she loses her freedom. And I was like, Oh god, I don't want that to be my story when I get married. I understood a lot of that, because that was what was being asked of me at that time, to give up, give up, give up, in order to be a woman to live in the society and be married and have a family.
<v Shamyla>是的。我记得当时想，梅格结婚时，他们有一整章关于她如何失去自由。我就想，哦，上帝，我不希望那成为我结婚时的故事。我非常理解其中的大部分内容，因为那时我正被要求这样做——放弃、放弃、放弃——以便成为一个在社会中生活、结婚并组建家庭的女人。

00:24:56.930 --> 00:25:13.980
<v Elna Baker>A friend at Shamyla's school told her she had a brother that Shamyla could marry, who would treat her well. So she began corresponding with him via her friend. But her family found out. And they were terrified that their reputation was going to be destroyed by it. Girls do not communicate directly with boys.
<v Elna Baker>沙米拉学校的一位朋友告诉她，她有个兄弟可以嫁给沙米拉，他会善待她。于是她开始通过朋友与他通信。但她的家人发现了。他们担心自己的名声会被毁掉。女孩不能直接与男孩交流。

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:26.110
<v Elna Baker>And they did what lots of families might do. They pulled her out of school and quickly started arranging her marriage to a 30-year-old man. As part of their damage control, they decided to send Shamyla away for a few weeks.
<v Elna Baker>他们做了许多家庭可能会做的事。他们让她退学，并迅速开始安排她与一名30岁男子的婚姻。作为损害控制的一部分，他们决定把沙米拉送走几周。

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:41.790
<v Elna Baker>But their only relatives who weren't in Peshawar were in America. So they made arrangements to send her back. And while she was there, her birth father and mother wanted her to learn new skills that would add to her bride price, the money the groom's family would be expected to pay them.
<v Elna Baker>但他们唯一不在白沙瓦的亲戚在美国。所以他们做了安排把她送回去。当她在那里时，她的亲生父母想让她学习新的技能，这将增加她的聘礼，即新郎家庭被预期付给他们的钱。

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:49.650
<v Shamyla>He sent me with a whole list. He was like, you have to learn driving, shorthand, swimming. [LAUGHING]
<v Shamyla>他给了我一整张清单。他说，你必须学习开车、速记、游泳。[大笑]

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:59.580
<v Elna Baker>It just seems like, after all the work they did to get you to stay, and also, their plans to marry you off, it just seems crazy that they would let you go back.
<v Elna Baker>这看起来，在他们做了那么多努力让你留下，并且计划把你嫁出去之后，他们让你回去看起来太疯狂了。

00:25:59.580 --> 00:26:19.530
<v Shamyla>Well it's funny, because she even said something crazy. She's like, if they're going to do anything to fix your face-- because remember, they cut my face open-- she's like, ask them if they can put a mole down there, like Cindy Crawford. And so I was like, OK, let's add mole to the list of things that will happen. [LAUGHING]
<v Shamyla>嗯，这很有趣，因为她甚至说了些疯狂的话。她说，如果要对你的脸做些什么修复——因为记住，他们割开了我的脸——她说，问问他们能不能在下面点个痣，像辛迪·克劳馥那样。所以我说，好吧，把痣加到将会发生的事情清单上。[大笑]

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:32.850
<v Elna Baker>They send her to stay with her US parents. Her birth father is so confident that Shamyla is one of them now, that she'll obediently return to Pakistan. He doesn't question letting her go. A female chaperone traveled with her.
<v Elna Baker>他们送她去和美国父母住在一起。她的亲生父亲非常自信沙米拉现在是他们的人了，她会顺从地回到巴基斯坦。他没有质疑让她回去。一位女性监护人陪同她旅行。

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:49.530
<v Shamyla>So we walked onto the tarmac. I climbed up in the plane. And as the plane took off, I just started crying nonstop. Because I knew I wasn't coming back. I just knew. I didn't know how I knew it. I don't know why I knew it. And I'm watching Pakistan slip away, and I'm like, I'm not going back here. It's never happening.
<v Shamyla>所以我们走上了停机坪。我爬上飞机。当飞机起飞时，我开始不停地哭。因为我知道我再也不会回来了。我只是知道。我不知道我怎么知道的。我不知道为什么我知道。看着巴基斯坦溜走，我想，我不会回到这里。这永远不会发生。

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:51.790
<v Elna Baker>And was it relief?
<v Elna Baker>那是解脱吗？

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:59.190
<v Shamyla>No. I was sad. I was really, really sad. And I was like, goodbye, life that I knew.
<v Shamyla>不。我很伤心。我真的、真的很伤心。我想，再见，我所知道的生活。

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:12.190
<v Elna Baker>At first, she had a hard time coming back to America. She was in total culture shock. She could hardly speak English. She didn't recognize her little brothers. The internet existed now. It was like she'd woken up from a coma.
<v Elna Baker>起初，她回到美国时度日艰难。她经历了完全的文化冲击。她几乎不会说英语。她认不出她的小兄弟们。互联网现在存在了。就像她从昏迷中醒来一样。

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:25.000
<v Shamyla>I remember thinking, I still like big hair. And everyone's like, that ended in 1989. No one does that anymore. And I was like, but I like that. They're like, no, now we straighten our hair. And I was like, I don't like that. Well, you have to.
<v Shamyla>我记得当时想，我还是喜欢蓬松的头发。每个人都说，那在1989年就结束了。没人再那样做了。我就说，但我喜欢那样。他们说，不，现在我们把头发拉直。我说，我不喜欢那样。嗯，你必须。

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:35.020
<v Elna Baker>She enrolled in community college. But she'd been so indoctrinated, it was hard for her to just switch it off. She'd see women in skirts and catch herself judging them.
<v Elna Baker>她进入社区大学就读。但她被洗脑太深了，很难一下子关掉它。她看到穿裙子的女性，会不由自主地评判她们。

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:45.760
<v Elna Baker>For the first few years, she was a mess. Trying to switch off the ideology was nearly impossible. She started to see a therapist, who helped her process the extent of what happened to her.
<v Elna Baker>最初几年，她一团糟。试图关掉这种意识形态几乎是不可能的。她开始看心理医生，这帮助她处理了发生在她身上的事情的程度。

00:27:45.760 --> 00:28:02.690
<v Elna Baker>There's something her mother said to me on the phone I can't stop thinking about. It's something she told her little sister once. Between you and me, we've ruined Shamyla's life.
<v Elna Baker>她母亲在电话里对我说了一些我无法停止思考的话。这是她曾经对她小妹妹说过的话。你我之间，我们毁了沙米拉的生活。

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:24.710
<v Elna Baker>It's been 23 years since she returned. Her life actually diverged from the second half of Little Women, the part she didn't like. She didn't get married, and she's independent. She runs her own practice as a therapist and social worker. She specifically works with people who suffer from trauma, because she understands it so well herself. But sometimes, she still returns to Little Women.
<v Elna Baker>她回来已经23年了。她的生活实际上偏离了《小妇人》的后半部分，即她不喜欢的那个部分。她没有结婚，并且很独立。她经营着自己的心理治疗和社工诊所。她专门与受创伤的人一起工作，因为她自己非常了解这一点。但有时，她还是会回到《小妇人》。

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:45.790
<v Shamyla>Well, it comes up in different situations. I'll be like, OK, so-- the other day, I actually-- this is funny. My friend has twins. And she was telling me, like, god, I'm so tired, and they don't listen, and my husband is so busy. And I referred her to the chapter of Meg trying to discipline her twins. And I was like, read this. This will help you. [LAUGHING]
<v Shamyla>嗯，它在不同的情况下会出现。我会想，好吧，那么——那天，实际上——这很有趣。我的朋友有双胞胎。她告诉我说，天哪，我太累了，他们不听话，我丈夫太忙了。我推荐她读梅格试图管教她的双胞胎那一章。我说，读这个。这会有帮助。[大笑]

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:52.160
<v Elna Baker>Wait, are you constantly-- do your friends have a joke or, like, Oh, not another Little Women reference.
<v Elna Baker>等等，你是不是一直在——你的朋友们有没有开玩笑，或者说，哦，又一个《小妇人》的引用。

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:58.570
<v Shamyla>No, I think that they think it's funny that I like the book so much. But I don't think anyone really understands why.
<v Shamyla>不，我想他们觉得我这么喜欢这本书很有趣。但我认为没人真正理解为什么。

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:03.320
<v Elna Baker>Wait, so it's like your only frame of reference?
<v Elna Baker>等等，所以这是你唯一的参考框架？

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:04.580
<v Shamyla>Yeah.
<v Shamyla>是的。

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:17.370
<v Elna Baker>When she's having a hard time or making a decision, she asks the question, opens the book, and whatever passage she reads, it's her answer from the March sisters. And she also has this ritual.
<v Elna Baker>当她遇到困难或做决定时，她会问问题，打开书，无论她读到哪一段，那都是马奇姐妹给她的答案。她还有一个仪式。

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:24.630
<v Shamyla>So every year on my birthday, I find the corresponding age that I am for the chapter I am, and I read that.
<v Shamyla>所以每年在我生日那天，我都会找到与我年龄相对应的章节，然后阅读它。

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:29.760
<v Elna Baker>In other words, chapter 30, when she turned 30; chapter 31, when she turned 31.
<v Elna Baker>换句话说，30岁时读第30章；31岁时读第31章。

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:35.460
<v Shamyla>To be like, so what is this life-- this year going to be about in my life? What's it telling me?
<v Shamyla>就是想，那么今年我的生活会是关于什么的？它在告诉我什么？

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:37.350
<v Elna Baker>So it's almost like your magic eight ball?
<v Elna Baker>所以它几乎就像你的魔法8号球？

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:40.350
<v Shamyla>Yeah, kind of. It's like my Bible.
<v Shamyla>是的，某种程度上。它就像我的圣经。

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:42.200
<v Elna Baker>What did you turn this year?
<v Elna Baker>你今年多少岁了？

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:43.260
<v Shamyla>I'm 41.
<v Shamyla>我41岁。

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:44.790
<v Elna Baker>And what chapter?
<v Elna Baker>那是哪一章？

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:51.570
<v Shamyla>Oh Amy-- it's called Learning to Forget. It's when Laurie's trying to forget Jo, and he starts falling in love with Amy.
<v Shamyla>哦，艾米——它叫学会遗忘。那是劳里试图忘记乔的时候，他开始爱上艾米。

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:54.300
<v Elna Baker>And how do you think that's going to relate to this year of be--
<v Elna Baker>你觉得这和你这一年——

00:29:54.300 --> 00:30:06.390
<v Shamyla>Maybe this year, finally someone's going to fall in love with me, [LAUGHING] the right one, not all the wrong ones.
<v Shamyla>也许今年，终于有人会爱上我了，[大笑]那个对的人，而不是所有错的人。

00:30:06.390 --> 00:30:35.990
<v Elna Baker>There are 47 chapters in Little Women. In six years, she'll turn 48. And she doesn't know what she'll do. She says maybe she'll start from the beginning again. And whereas the book used to be a way of escaping her room in Pakistan, now it's a way of returning to that world she built alone in her room. It's as if the March sisters are all still living there, and she's the one who left them behind.
<v Elna Baker>《小妇人》有47章。六年内，她将满48岁。她不知道她会怎么做。她说也许她会从头再读一遍。而这本书过去是逃离她在巴基斯坦房间的一种方式，现在则是回到她在房间里独自构建的那个世界的一种方式。就好像马奇姐妹都还住在那里，而她是那个把她们留在身后的人。

00:30:35.990 --> 00:31:06.030
<v Ira Glass>Elna Baker is one of the producers of our show. Shamyla told a version of this story herself on the storytelling podcast "Risk," which tapes for a live audience. You can hear that version and all those stories at risk-show.com. Coming up, Wu Tang Is For The Children, children are the future. So is Wu Tang the future? That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>埃尔娜·贝克是我们节目的制作人之一。沙米拉自己在故事讲述播客“Risk”中讲述了这个故事的版本，该播客有现场观众。你可以在risk-show.com收听那个版本和所有那些故事。接下来，武当是为了孩子们，孩子是未来。那么武当是未来吗？稍后，来自芝加哥公共广播，当我们的节目继续。

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:24.450
<v Ira Glass>This is "This American Life." I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show-- "The Weight of Words," stories about the words that people turn to for comfort or strength. We first aired this show in 2019. We've arrived at Act 2 of our show, Act 2-- Daddy Lessons.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们选择一个主题，带给你关于那个主题的不同种类的故事。今天的节目——“文字的分量”，关于人们求助于获得安慰或力量的文字的故事。我们在2019年首次播出了这个节目。我们来到了节目的第二幕，第二幕——爸爸的教训。

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:48.950
<v Ira Glass>So the most recent bit of serious parenting that my dad did with me-- and I say this, realizing that this really might be the very last life lesson he might give me; we are both getting up there in years-- was a protracted month-long campaign to switch from Excel to Quicken, to keep track of my personal finances. He felt very strongly about this. And I just want to say, yes, the numbers add up the same, no matter what software you use.
<v Ira Glass>所以，我父亲最近对我进行的最重要的一次教导——我说这句话时意识到，这确实可能是他给我的最后的人生教训；我们俩年龄都大了——是一场持续一个月的长篇大论，为了让我从Excel切换到Quicken，以记录我的个人财务。他对此感觉非常强烈。我只想说，是的，无论你使用什么软件，数字相加的结果是一样的。

00:31:48.950 --> 00:32:05.540
<v Ira Glass>But Quicken, I mean, don't get my dad started. He's a retired CPA. He had already trained me to save all my receipts. Please, that's basic. And he went on with this at such length, that at some point, I realized, Oh, switching software was not a piece of friendly financial advice. This was actual parenting happening.
<v Ira Glass>但Quicken，我的意思是，别让我的父亲开始谈这个。他是一位退休的注册会计师。他已经训练我保存所有的收据。拜托，那是基础。他对此滔滔不绝，以至于在某个时刻，我意识到，哦，切换软件不是友好的财务建议。这是实际发生的育儿行为。

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:28.900
<v Ira Glass>Not switching to Quicken meant an actual repudiation of him as a father. It would actually be a failure of parenthood. Parents try to shape who we are in their own image so often. They make sure their kids are exposed to whatever-- modern art, sports, nature, science, theater, whatever they love. They train them to be the world's greatest tennis player or golfer, that kind of thing.
<v Ira Glass>不切换到Quicken意味着对我作为父亲的他的一种实际否定。那实际上将是一种育儿上的失败。父母经常试图按照他们自己的形象塑造我们。他们确保他们的孩子接触任何东西——现代艺术、体育、自然、科学、戏剧，任何他们热爱的东西。他们训练他们成为世界上最伟大的网球运动员或高尔夫球手之类的人。

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:31.180
<v Neil Drumming>My friend Adam came up with a scheme like that.
<v Neil Drumming>我的朋友亚当想出了一个那样的计划。

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:35.200
<v Ira Glass>This is Neil Drumming. His friend Adam is Adam Mansbach, a writer.
<v Ira Glass>这是尼尔·德鲁明。他的朋友亚当是亚当·曼斯巴赫，一位作家。

00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:42.460
<v Neil Drumming>Adam is a DJ, a record collector, and he wanted his daughter Vivien to love music the way he loved music, exactly the way he loved music.
<v Neil Drumming>亚当是一位DJ、唱片收藏家，他希望他的女儿维维安能像他热爱音乐那样热爱音乐，完全按照他热爱音乐的方式。

00:32:42.460 --> 00:33:05.830
<v Adam Mansbach>One of the things I feel like I got right about parenting is that I never played Viv any kids music. Because I understood that any song that you played a two-year-old, a three-year-old, might become their favorite song, and then you would have to listen to it 20 times a day. So I never played her anything I wasn't willing to listen to 20 times a day. So she, from a very young age, was listening to a lot of music I liked.
<v Adam Mansbach>我觉得我在育儿方面做对的一件事是，我从不放儿童音乐给维维听。因为我明白，你放给两岁、三岁孩子听的任何歌，都可能成为他们最喜欢的歌，然后你就得每天听20遍。所以我从不放我不愿意每天听20遍的东西。所以她从小就听很多我喜欢的音乐。

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:10.810
<v Neil Drumming>Is that a thing that you got right about parenting? Or is that a thing that you just did for yourself?
<v Neil Drumming>那是一件你做对的育儿事吗？还是你只是为你自己做的？

00:33:10.810 --> 00:33:21.510
<v Adam Mansbach>I mean, [LAUGHING] I mean, aren't the two synonymous really, Neil? I'm not sure what the difference is.
<v Adam Mansbach>我的意思是，[大笑]我的意思是，尼尔，这两者不是同义词吗？我不确定有什么区别。

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:33.030
<v Neil Drumming>Adam likes '80s and '90s hip hop perhaps more than me, which is saying a lot. Anyway, he fed Vivien a steady diet of classic material-- A Tribe Called Quest, Public Enemy, Run-DMC. Das EFX.
<v Neil Drumming>亚当喜欢80年代和90年代的嘻哈音乐可能比我还多，这说明了很多。总之，他给维维安喂了一份稳定的经典材料——探索部落组合、人民公敌、Run-DMC。Das EFX。

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:49.740
<v Adam Mansbach>There was probably a year, probably two years, where the Stetsasonic record In Full Gear got more play in our house than any other record. We may be the only household in North America that can say that, that in the year 2014, we listened to In Full Gear more than any other album.
<v Adam Mansbach>可能有一年，甚至两年，Stetsasonic的唱片《In Full Gear》在我们家播放的次数比任何其他唱片都多。我们可能是北美唯一能这么说的家庭，即在2014年，我们听《In Full Gear》的次数超过了任何其他专辑。

00:33:49.740 --> 00:33:51.870
<v Neil Drumming>[LAUGHING] What is that record, '88?
<v Neil Drumming>[大笑]那张唱片是什么，88年的？

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:57.750
<v Adam Mansbach>Yeah, it came out in '88.
<v Adam Mansbach>是的，它是88年出的。

00:33:57.750 --> 00:34:14.430
<v Neil Drumming>This exposure therapy approach to parenting has been known to fail. I grew up surrounded by beds that were always made. But if you walked into my apartment now, you'd see no evidence of that. In Adam's case, though, it worked. He noticed that by the time she was six years old, Vivien could rap along to any hip hop song you played her.
<v Neil Drumming>这种接触疗法式的育儿方式众所周知会失败。我是在总是铺好的床周围长大的。但如果你现在走进我的公寓，你不会看到任何证据。但在亚当的案例中，它起作用了。他注意到当她六岁时，维维安可以随着你播放的任何嘻哈歌曲一起说唱。

00:34:14.430 --> 00:34:37.170
<v Adam Mansbach>She would memorize it, and she would be very much in the pocket and just not missing a beat. I remember one of the songs that we played a lot was The Choice is Yours," remixed by Black Sheep. I remember recording her just on my phone, doing the third verse of the song, the famous third verse of the song, Engine Engine Number 9.
<v Adam Mansbach>她会把它背下来，而且非常有节奏感，不会错过任何一个节拍。我记得我们播放很多的一首歌是Black Sheep混音的《The Choice is Yours》。我记得录下她在我手机上表演这首歌的第三段，著名的第三段，Engine Engine Number 9。

00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:45.420
<v Vivien>If my train goes off the track, pick it up, pick it up, pick it up. Back on the seat, crispy and clean. You can try, but know what--
<v Vivien>如果我的火车脱轨，把它捡起来，把它捡起来，把它捡起来。回到座位上，干脆利落。你可以试试，但知道什么——

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:59.540
<v Adam Mansbach>And she had it perfectly down. And it's not an easy verse to do. And I remember sending it to our friend Jay Period, who in turn sent it to Dres, from Black Sheep. And he was like, Yo, this is incredible. Like, tell her-- give her my props.
<v Adam Mansbach>她完全掌握了。而且那不是一段容易完成的。我记得把它发给我们朋友Jay Period，他转而把它发给了Black Sheep的Dres。他说，哟，这太不可思议了。告诉她——替我向她致敬。

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:07.650
<v Adam Mansbach>And I was like, Yo, Viv, you're six, so this means nothing to you. But Dres from Black Sheep said that you're dope. This is his song.
<v Adam Mansbach>我说，哟，维维，你才六岁，所以这对你来说没什么意义。但Black Sheep的Dres说你很棒。这是他的歌。

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:08.030
<v Adam Mansbach>[MUSIC - "THE CHOICE IS YOURS" BY BLACK SHEEP]
<v Adam Mansbach>[音乐 - Black Sheep的《THE CHOICE IS YOURS》]

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:17.340
<v Adam Mansbach>(RAPPING) Engine, engine number 9, on the New York transit line, if my train goes off the track, pick it up, pick it up, pick it up. Come on.
<v Adam Mansbach>(说唱) 引擎，引擎9号，在纽约公交线上，如果我的火车脱轨，把它捡起来，把它捡起来，把它捡起来。来吧。

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:28.120
<v Neil Drumming>Like a lot of guys our age, Adam tried his hand at being a rapper when he was younger. He joined a group. They made some songs that never quite went anywhere. He stayed in the hip hop world, though. He still has a lot of connections.
<v Neil Drumming>像我们这个年纪的很多人一样，亚当年轻时尝试过做说唱歌手。他加入了一个团体。他们制作了一些从没成名的歌。但他留在了嘻哈世界。他仍然有很多关系。

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:43.460
<v Neil Drumming>So he was thrilled to find out his daughter had some skills. And proud father that he is, he wanted more people to know. When another one of his entertainment industry buddies needed a theme song for a PBS Kids show he was making, called "Bug Bites," Adam jumped at the chance to write Vivien some original material.
<v Neil Drumming>所以他很激动地发现女儿有一些技巧。作为自豪的父亲，他想让更多人知道。当他的另一位娱乐行业好友需要他制作的一个PBS儿童节目《Bug Bites》的主题曲时，亚当抓住了这个机会为维维安创作了一些原创材料。

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:43.800
<v Neil Drumming>[MUSIC - "BUG BITES"]
<v Neil Drumming>[音乐 - 《BUG BITES》]

00:35:43.800 --> 00:35:57.450
<v Vivien>(RAPPING) Six legs, eight legs, fly and crawl. Let's get it, world, come one, come all. Bugs can teach us so much more about the world. Let's go explore! Some of these facts are incredible knowing, like bees keep flowers and vegetables growing.
<v Vivien>(说唱) 六条腿，八条腿，飞和爬。让我们得到它，世界，来吧，全部来吧。虫子能教我们更多关于世界的事。去探索吧！其中一些事实令人难以置信，比如蜜蜂让花卉和蔬菜生长。

00:35:57.450 --> 00:36:03.810
<v Neil Drumming>The show wound up not using the song. But Adam couldn't stop listening to it. He walked around playing it over and over in his headphones.
<v Neil Drumming>这个节目最终没用这首歌。但亚当听个不停。他戴着耳机一遍又一遍地播放它。

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:17.100
<v Adam Mansbach>I mean, I knew she was skilled. But I sort of took it for granted. I was like, oh, that's just regular. Everybody can rhyme. Everybody I know can rhyme, you know? But I remember at that point being like, oh, you know what? This is really unique.
<v Adam Mansbach>我的意思是，我知道她很熟练。但我把它当成了理所当然。我想，哦，那只是常规。每个人都能押韵。我认识的每个人都能押韵，你知道吗？但我记得那一刻心想，哦，你知道吗？这真的很独特。

00:36:17.100 --> 00:36:22.410
<v Neil Drumming>Did this change your expectations for what this could be?
<v Neil Drumming>这改变了你对这可能会成为什么的期望吗？

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:28.530
<v Adam Mansbach>Yeah, I think it opened-- it opened it up and made me want to maybe want to do more and see where we could take this.
<v Adam Mansbach>是的，我想它打开了——它打开了局面，让我想要做更多，看看我们能把这个带到哪里。

00:36:28.530 --> 00:36:29.520
<v Neil Drumming>And so they did.
<v Neil Drumming>于是他们就这样做了。

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.790
<v Vivien>Step into my office and have a seat.
<v Vivien>走进我的办公室，坐下来。

00:36:31.790 --> 00:36:50.310
<v Neil Drumming>He and Vivien would plan out songs during the winters in California and record with his music industry friends back East during the summers. Writing for Vivien, Adam would layer in little in-jokes they shared or references that he knew his daughter would appreciate. Like when she was into Percy Jackson, he'd weave in the Greek gods. And when she was into Harry Potter, he worked that in.
<v Neil Drumming>他和维维安会在加州的冬天计划歌曲，并在夏天和他在东部的音乐行业朋友一起录音。为维维安写作时，亚当会层层叠叠地加入他们共享的小冷笑话或他知道女儿会欣赏的参考。就像她迷上珀西·杰克逊时，他会织入希腊神话。当她迷上哈利·波特时，他把它写了进去。

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:54.060
<v Adam Mansbach>Yeah, I'm nice with my, like, Dumbledore, so what you want to rumble for?
<v Adam Mansbach>是的，我的邓布利多很棒，所以你为什么要吵闹？

00:36:54.060 --> 00:37:07.210
<v Neil Drumming>Vivien recorded under the moniker The Jazz Wolf, a name that she came up with. Her first real taste of mainstream exposure came by way of a song that Adam, who's originally from Massachusetts, wrote for her to perform in a hometown Boston accent.
<v Neil Drumming>维维安以“爵士狼”的名义录音，这是她自己想出的名字。她对主流曝光的第一次真正尝试是通过一首歌，亚当（原籍马萨诸塞州）写让她用波士顿家乡口音表演。

00:37:07.210 --> 00:37:07.700
<v Neil Drumming>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Neil Drumming>[播放音乐]

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:21.720
<v Vivien>(RAPPING) My name's Clarissa, I'm wicked pissah. That girl Amber, I had to dis her. She opened up a powder keg. You're speaking chowderhead. I'm a hot target. You work at Star Market. Nobody [INAUDIBLE].
<v Vivien>(说唱) 我叫克拉丽莎，我坏透了。那个女孩琥珀，我不得不斥责她。她打开了一个火药桶。你是个蠢货。我是个热门目标。你在Star Market工作。没有人[听不清]。

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:38.550
<v Neil Drumming>They made a video for the song, which made it onto the home page of the popular humor website Funny or Die. But "Much Lobstah" was more like a comedy bit than a banger, more DJ Jazzy Jeff and the Fresh Prince than it was Eric B and Rakim. Adam didn't feel the song was representative of how he imagined Vivien as an artist.
<v Neil Drumming>他们为这首歌制作了一个视频，登上了热门幽默网站Funny or Die的主页。但《Much Lobstah》更像是一个喜剧片段而不是热门单曲，更多是DJ Jazzy Jeff和Fresh Prince风格，而不是Eric B和Rakim风格。亚当觉得这首歌并不代表他作为艺术家想象中的维维安。

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:52.820
<v Adam Mansbach>It was a funny song, but it was a funny song. It wasn't truly indicative of her skills as an emcee, because it was more focused on dropping Massachusetts-centric jokes.
<v Adam Mansbach>这是一首有趣的歌，但它只是首有趣的歌。它并不真正代表她作为MC的技巧，因为它更关注抛出马萨诸塞州中心的笑话。

00:37:52.820 --> 00:38:01.940
<v Vivien>(RAPPING) Eat a grinder, drink a beer. Then I slap a critic. You're off the rails like Teddy was at Chappaquiddick. [BLAST]
<v Vivien>(说唱) 吃个研磨机，喝杯啤酒。然后我给评论家一巴掌。你像泰迪在查帕奎迪克那样脱轨了。[爆炸]

00:38:01.940 --> 00:38:19.790
<v Neil Drumming>Adam had told me that his kid rapped early on. But I didn't understand how serious the two of them were about this until September of 2017, when I received an email from Adam with no subject heading. Attached to the email were two remarkably well-produced songs, described in the body of the email simply as, Jazz Wolf Heat Rocks.
<v Neil Drumming>亚当告诉我他孩子很早就在说唱。但在2017年9月我收到亚当发的一封没有主题的邮件之前，我不明白他们俩对这个有多认真。邮件附件里是两首制作得非常好的歌，在邮件正文中简述为“爵士狼热石”。

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:31.020
<v Neil Drumming>One of these so-called Heat Rocks featured Vivien, who I had met once only briefly, but knew to be a small, blond White girl, rapping over a fluttery dancehall rhythm in full-on Jamaican patois.
<v Neil Drumming>其中一首所谓的热石特色是维维安，我只见过她一次，但知道是一个个子娇小、金发的白人女孩，在飘忽的舞厅节奏上用纯正的牙买加方言说唱。

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:31.480
<v Neil Drumming>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Neil Drumming>[播放音乐]

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:45.710
<v Vivien>(RAPPING) Jazz Wolf for better rhyme, sound girl better rhyme, get your gun, where you come, fire bun, get it done. Should we test it? None. We are [INAUDIBLE]. Champion. A champion. Swim over land and walk the ocean. Sound girl, we take apart in slow motion.
<v Vivien>(说唱) 爵士狼更好的押韵，音响女孩更好的押韵，拿上你的枪，你来自哪里，开火，把它做好。我们应该测试一下吗？没有。我们是[听不清]。冠军。冠军。跨越陆地游泳，走过海洋。音响女孩，我们把它慢动作分解。

00:38:45.710 --> 00:39:09.770
<v Neil Drumming>Did listening to this reggae song, penned by a best-selling Jewish author for his nine-year-old and most likely, recorded in or near his house on Martha's Vineyard, make me feel uncomfortable? Yeah, sure, a little. But I've been listening to hip hop a long time. And between all the sampling and biting and flipping styles, I've kind of made my peace with cultural appropriation. All I ask is that you give credit where credit is due and that you do your due diligence.
<v Neil Drumming>听这首由一位畅销犹太作家为他九岁孩子创作的雷鬼歌曲，而且很可能是在他家附近录制的，让我感到不舒服吗？是的，当然，有点。但我听嘻哈音乐很久了。在所有的采样、剽窃和翻转风格之间，我已经与文化挪用达成了和解。我所要求的一切就是你要给予应有的认可，并且你要做好尽职调查。

00:39:09.770 --> 00:39:15.590
<v Adam Mansbach>You know, Neil, I should say that I had some misgivings about the song.
<v Adam Mansbach>你知道，尼尔，我应该说我对这首歌有些顾虑。

