<v Neil Drumming>[LAUGHING] I was going to ask.
<v Neil Drumming>[大笑]我正想问。

00:39:17.570 --> 00:39:23.210
<v Adam Mansbach>I definitely-- really, the first thing I did was send it to every Jamaican I knew.
<v Adam Mansbach>我绝对——我做的第一件事就是把它发给我认识的每一个牙买加人。

00:39:23.210 --> 00:39:23.930
<v Adam Mansbach>[LAUGHTER]
<v Adam Mansbach>[大笑]

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:28.520
<v Adam Mansbach>I'd be like, yo, so what do you think about this?
<v Adam Mansbach>我会说，哟，那你对这个怎么看？

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:44.120
<v Neil Drumming>Anyway, the point is, it wasn't just me and some Caribbean Islanders who got this email. Adam was stepping up Vivien's game and had sent the Jazz Wolf de facto demo to just about every music industry contact he had. To a certain degree, it worked. Friends praised his daughter's music.
<v Neil Drumming>总之，关键是，不仅仅是我和一些加勒比岛民收到了这封邮件。亚当在提升维维安的水平，并把爵士狼的事实演示发给了他所有的音乐行业联系人。在某种程度上，它起作用了。朋友们称赞他女儿的音乐。

00:39:44.120 --> 00:40:11.890
<v Neil Drumming>Artists who Adam admired wanted to record with her. A big player in the music industry who had a hand in discovering Lauryn Hill suggested that they record more songs for a legitimate demo. Bolstered by all this interest, Adam set out to write Vivien something really special. It was the kind of thing he'd have written for himself back in his rapping days-- multi-syllabic, rapid fire, a real showpiece, designed to take the Jazz Wolf all the way to the next level. He was on a business trip, when the ghost writer in him really took over.
<v Neil Drumming>亚当钦佩的艺术家想和她录音。一位曾参与发现劳琳·希尔的音乐行业大人物建议他们录制更多的歌作为正式演示。在所有这些兴趣的激励下，亚当着手为维维安写一些真正特别的东西。这是他当年自己说唱时会写的东西——多音节、快速火炮、真正的展示品，旨在将爵士狼带到下一个水平。他出差时，他心中的代笔人接管了工作。

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:30.570
<v Adam Mansbach>And I remember I had to go away for a week. I was in Chicago, doing some stuff. And I writing bars for her. And I was really trying to write the most complicated, difficult verses I could. Because I knew she could handle it, and I was like, this is going to be really fun.
<v Adam Mansbach>我记得我得离开一周。我在芝加哥，做些事。我正在为她写词。我确实在努力写出我能写出的最复杂、最困难的段落。因为我知道她能应付，我想，这会非常有趣。

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:41.150
<v Adam Mansbach>And I came back with the song. And we rehearsed it for a long time. We got a beat from Dug Infinite, an illustrious producer, who worked with Common and all kinds of people. And Viv had been practicing it for weeks.
<v Adam Mansbach>我带着歌回来了。我们排练了很长时间。我们从杰出的制作人Dug Infinite那里得到了一段节拍，他曾与Common和各种各样的人合作。维维已经练习了几周了。

00:40:41.150 --> 00:41:01.890
<v Adam Mansbach>And we had plans to go to his house and record it. She was nine at the time, I remember, because the chorus of the song played with the fact that she was nine. We flipped the chorus from nine millimeter goes bang. And it was like, wa da da dang, wa da da da dang. Hey, listen, 'cause I'm nine, and I kick enough slang.
<v Adam Mansbach>我们计划去他家录音。记得她那时九岁，因为歌的合唱部分玩了一个梗，关于她九岁。我们翻转了《9毫米响个不停》的合唱部分。就像，哒哒哒，哒哒哒。嘿，听着，因为我九岁了，而且我踢得够多了。

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:07.640
<v Neil Drumming>But despite all Adam's craft in preparation, there was an unforeseen, but not unforeseeable issue with the talent.
<v Neil Drumming>但尽管亚当精心制作和准备，在人才方面出现了一个未预见到但并非不可预见的问题。

00:41:07.640 --> 00:41:20.300
<v Adam Mansbach>She hadn't slept that night. I think we had a new baby. Viv has two younger sisters, and the older of the two at that time was only a few months old and was up, crying. And so Viv was on no sleep.
<v Adam Mansbach>她那天晚上没睡。我想我们有个新生儿。维维有两个更小的妹妹，两个妹妹中较大的那个当时才几个月大，一直在哭。所以维维根本没睡觉。

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:37.580
<v Adam Mansbach>And I remember we got to the studio really early. And she was just exhausted and not really at her peak. And it was difficult for her to get it down. And we kind of called it. We cut it short. I was like, you know what? Let's come back another day, Dug, because this isn't it.
<v Adam Mansbach>我记得我们很早就到了录音室。她只是精疲力竭，没达到巅峰。这让她很难完成。我们算是取消了。我说，你知道吗？Dug，我们改天再来吧，因为这不行。

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:38.780
<v Vivien>I'm sorry.
<v Vivien>对不起。

00:41:38.780 --> 00:41:39.250
<v Adam Mansbach>[LAUGHING]
<v Adam Mansbach>[大笑]

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:41.530
<v Vivien>No, I'm sorry. I didn't mean to be like that.
<v Vivien>不，对不起。我不是那个意思。

00:41:41.530 --> 00:41:44.240
<v Adam Mansbach>Nah, are you kidding me? It's not your fault.
<v Adam Mansbach>不，你在开玩笑吗？那不是你的错。

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:49.300
<v Neil Drumming>That's Vivien. She was in the studio with her dad during this interview. She's 11 now.
<v Neil Drumming>那是维维安。在这次采访中，她和父亲在录音室里。她现在11岁了。

00:41:49.300 --> 00:42:07.690
<v Adam Mansbach>Right, so I remember coming back from there. And Viv was tired. And in the car, she told me. She was like, Papa, you know, I'm just not that into this right now, something along those lines. Like, I'm not into you writing me these rap songs right now.
<v Adam Mansbach>对，所以我记得从那里回来。维维很累。在车里，她告诉了我。她说，爸爸，你知道，我现在真的不太想做这个，大意是这样。就像，我现在不想让你给我写这些说唱歌曲了。

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:10.510
<v Neil Drumming>Vivian, do you remember this moment at all, this night?
<v Neil Drumming>维维安，你还记得这一刻吗，这个晚上？

00:42:10.510 --> 00:42:33.900
<v Vivien>I did. And I think that the reason I told him then was that-- this is going to sound really stupid. But I thought that because-- I think he would be less angry if he knew I was tired and didn't want to argue.
<v Vivien>我记得。我想我当时告诉他是因为——这听起来会很蠢。但我认为，因为——我想如果他知道我很累而且不想争论，他会没那么生气。

00:42:33.900 --> 00:42:37.280
<v Neil Drumming>So you had already come to this decision?
<v Neil Drumming>所以你已经做出了这个决定？

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:42.270
<v Vivien>Yeah. I mean, we hadn't talked about it, but I'd already gone through it in my head what I was trying to say.
<v Vivien>是的。我的意思是，我们没讨论过，但我已经在脑子里经历过我想说的话。

00:42:42.270 --> 00:42:54.530
<v Neil Drumming>OK, let's back up a bit. Vivien's dissatisfaction with how Adam was steeling her rap career goes back long before this night, back many car rides, in fact. She recalls trying to listen to new music while riding with her dad.
<v Neil Drumming>好吧，让我们往回退一点。维维安对亚当如何为她打造说唱事业的不满，追溯到那个晚上之前很久，事实上是许多次车程。她回想起当和爸爸一起开车时尝试听新音乐。

00:42:54.530 --> 00:43:03.380
<v Vivien>Just flipping through radio stations in the car and him turning on a radio station, and I was like, oh yeah, this one. He's like, Oh, that's hot trash. We're not listening to that.
<v Vivien>只是在车里翻电台，他打开一个电台，我当时想，哦对了，这个。他说，哦，那是热垃圾。我们不听那个。

00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:25.130
<v Neil Drumming>You see, Adam didn't realize this, but even while she'd been sharing his love of hip hop, Vivien had been developing her own taste. She'd started to enjoy more contemporary music, God forbid, even pop music-- Taylor Swift, and later, Kesha and Nicki Minaj and Cardi B. But she'd been listening to music through Adam's ears long enough to understand and internalize just how much he wouldn't approve.
<v Neil Drumming>你看，亚当没意识到这一点，但即使维维安一直分享他对嘻哈的热爱，她也一直在培养自己的品味。她开始享受更现代的音乐，天哪，甚至流行音乐——泰勒·斯威夫特，后来是Kesha、妮琪·米娜和卡迪·B。但她听音乐透过亚当的耳朵听得够久，足以理解并内化他会有多不赞同。

00:43:25.130 --> 00:43:31.450
<v Neil Drumming>So you felt like there were things that you liked that you couldn't tell him about?
<v Neil Drumming>所以你觉得有些你喜欢的东西你不能告诉他？

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:57.340
<v Vivien>No. Well, there were things that I liked that I could tell him about, but that I didn't feel like there was any point to telling him about, because he would tell me all the errors. And I would be like, yo, like, listen to this person's voice. Or like, this song is so good; and play it. And then afterward, Papa would be like, you want to hear something good? And run into where he keeps his records, and I'm like, oh, gosh.
<v Vivien>不。嗯，有些东西我喜欢但可以告诉他，但我觉得告诉他没有任何意义，因为他会告诉我所有的错误。我会说，哟，就像，听听这个人的声音。或者说，这首歌太好了；然后播放。然后之后，爸爸会说，想听点好的吗？然后跑到他存放唱片的地方，我就想，哦，天哪。

00:43:57.340 --> 00:44:00.310
<v Neil Drumming>Do you remember any examples of this?
<v Neil Drumming>你记得这方面的任何例子吗？

00:44:00.310 --> 00:44:16.390
<v Vivien>Uh, one time, I was playing him a song from Hamilton, where someone was rapping really fast. And I played it, and he just half listened. And then afterward, he's like, you want to hear really fast rapping?
<v Vivien>呃，有一次，我在给他放《汉密尔顿》里的一首歌，里面有人说唱得很快。我放了，他只是半听着。然后之后，他说，你想听听真正快速的说唱吗？

00:44:16.390 --> 00:44:17.290
<v Vivien>[LAUGHTER]
<v Vivien>[大笑]

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:18.460
<v Vivien>And I'm like, oh god.
<v Vivien>我心想，哦，上帝。

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:20.590
<v Neil Drumming>What did you play her, Adam?
<v Neil Drumming>你给她放了什么，亚当？

00:44:20.590 --> 00:44:23.530
<v Adam Mansbach>Well, all right. Let me back up for a second.
<v Adam Mansbach>好吧，好吧。让我退后一步。

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:25.060
<v Vivien>Sorry, Papa.
<v Vivien>对不起，爸爸。

00:44:25.060 --> 00:44:40.900
<v Adam Mansbach>At the risk of getting on my soapbox and with the full understanding, this is never going to make it actually into this piece, my thing with Hamilton was like, cool. Hamilton is fine. My issue with it is that a billion people who have no interest--
<v Adam Mansbach>冒着站上肥皂箱的风险，并且完全理解这一点永远不会真的进入这篇报道，我对《汉密尔顿》的感觉就像是，酷。汉密尔顿很好。我的问题是，十亿个没兴趣的人——

00:44:40.900 --> 00:45:02.670
<v Neil Drumming>Here, Adam's critique of Hamilton, while spirited, is mostly remarkable, in that he somehow manages to vehemently object to the musical's popularity while simultaneously namedropping his friendship with one of its former stars, Daveed Diggs. Vivien didn't just want to feel free to listen to Hamilton in the car on the way to school. She wanted to incorporate all of her newfound influences into her own music.
<v Neil Drumming>这里，亚当对《汉密尔顿》的批评，虽然很有精神，但大多引人注目的是，他竟然一方面强烈反对这部音乐剧的流行，一方面同时顺带提到他与其中一位前明星戴维德·迪格斯的友谊。维维安不仅想在去学校的车上自由地听《汉密尔顿》。她想将她所有新发现的影响力融入到自己的音乐中。

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:15.120
<v Vivien>I felt like-- I mean, it's really cool that I'm doing all Papa's cool stuff. But if I have this voice, and I have access to this recording, can't I use it to do things that are more my style?
<v Vivien>但说唱是进入它的方式？

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:34.310
<v Neil Drumming>It's not all that surprising that someone Vivien's age would eventually diverge from the path her father set her on when she was six. And this wasn't even the first time Vivien had stood up to her dad. She's usually pretty outspoken. But she told me this time was different, that she had been more scared than usual to speak her mind and that it took her months to work up the courage.
<v Neil Drumming>维维安这个年纪的人最终会偏离她父亲在她六岁时为她设定的道路，这并不奇怪。这甚至不是维维安第一次反抗她爸爸。她通常很直言不讳。但她告诉我这次不同，她比平时更害怕表达自己的想法，她花了几个月才鼓起勇气。

00:45:34.310 --> 00:45:43.140
<v Neil Drumming>She was right to be concerned. Adam took it noticeably hard. To hear him tell it, he interpreted this slight rejection as a sort of referendum on his parenting.
<v Neil Drumming>她担心得有道理。亚当受到了明显的打击。在他看来，他将这种轻微的拒绝视为对他育儿的一种公投。

00:45:43.140 --> 00:46:33.410
<v Adam Mansbach>I felt a certain kind of sadness on a couple of levels-- first, disappointment in myself for getting carried away and making her feel uncomfortable or making her feel like an unwilling participant in this project. And also, just a sense of letdown that wow, maybe we were no longer going to be hanging out after dinner, playing instrumentals, and fitting rhymes to them, and practicing, and rehearsing, and cutting scratch demos, and that kind of thing. Because it was something that brought me a lot of joy, both reveling in her skill, but really more than anything, just spending time together, and that feeling of collaboration-- which is wonderful when you find it with anybody. But when you find it with your own kid, it's magical.
<v Adam Mansbach>我在几个层面上感到一种悲伤——首先，对自己感到失望，因为太得意忘形，让她感到不舒服，或者让她感觉自己是这个项目的非自愿参与者。还有一种失落感，哇，也许我们以后不再会晚餐后待在一起，播放伴奏，填入押韵，练习，排练，剪辑刮盘演示，之类的事情了。因为那是一件给我带来很多快乐的事，既沉浸在她的技巧中，但更重要的是，只是花时间在一起，那种协作的感觉——当你和任何人找到这种感觉时都很美妙。但当你和自己的孩子找到这种感觉时，那是魔法。

00:46:33.410 --> 00:46:46.030
<v Neil Drumming>I mean, this is me, like armchair psychotherapist. But was there any part of you that was deflecting some energy into this in a way that maybe was more for you than for Vivien?
<v Neil Drumming>我的意思是，这是我，像个业余心理治疗师。但你有没有一部分能量是以某种可能更多是为了你而不是为了维维安的方式在偏转？

00:46:46.030 --> 00:46:54.700
<v Adam Mansbach>That's a great question. I mean, the whole period of time that we're talking about is fairly tumultuous. I mean, from when Viv was six until now--
<v Adam Mansbach>这是个好问题。我的意思是，我们谈论的整个时期相当动荡。我的意思是，从维维六岁到现在——

00:46:54.700 --> 00:46:55.390
<v Vivien>Five years.
<v Vivien>五年。

00:46:55.390 --> 00:47:17.200
<v Adam Mansbach>Five years-- I mean, that's a period of time that covers her mom and I splitting up. It covers a new relationship, two new babies. So yeah, you probably have a point there, I mean, at least in the sense of this being something that was purely fun and exciting and didn't feel heavy in any way.
<v Adam Mansbach>五年——我的意思是，那段时间涵盖了她妈妈和我分开。它涵盖了一段新恋情，两个新生儿。所以是的，你可能说得对，我的意思是，至少在某种意义上，这是一种纯粹有趣、令人兴奋且没有任何负担的东西。

00:47:17.200 --> 00:47:24.980
<v Neil Drumming>They never did finish that song. Adam and Vivien's magical collaboration was not infinitely sustainable. But so what?
<v Neil Drumming>他们确实没完成那首歌。亚当和维维安的魔法协作不是无限可持续的。但那又怎样？

00:47:24.980 --> 00:47:52.220
<v Vivien>When everything was happening in our family, also, it was a way for me to connect with Papa. And also, after we'd had a lot of fun kicking rhymes, we could talk about the more important stuff. He could be like, how do you feel? How about the new baby? And I feel like it just-- I would be a lot less close with Papa now if we hadn't spent so much time together doing that. I wouldn't be coming into your room and be like, let's talk about this.
<v Vivien>当家里发生这一切时，这也成了我与爸爸联系的一种方式。而且，在我们在踢押韵中玩得很开心之后，我们可以谈论更重要的事情。他可以说，你感觉如何？那个新生儿怎么样？我觉得它只是——如果不是因为我们花了那么多时间在一起做那个，我现在会离爸爸远得多。我就不会走进你的房间说，我们谈谈这个。

00:47:52.220 --> 00:47:54.920
<v Adam Mansbach>But the rapping was the way into it?
<v Adam Mansbach>但说唱是进入其中的途径吗？

00:47:54.920 --> 00:47:58.700
<v Vivien>Yeah.
<v Vivien>是的。

00:47:58.700 --> 00:48:06.200
<v Adam Mansbach>That makes me happy. I'm glad to hear that.
<v Adam Mansbach>那让我很高兴。很高兴听到这个。

00:48:06.200 --> 00:48:16.340
<v Neil Drumming>Recently, Vivien has been getting more into singing. She writes her own poems and adapts them into songs. She's written a few, and she thinks she could be good at it with a little time a little help.
<v Neil Drumming>最近，维维安越来越喜欢唱歌了。她写自己的诗并把它们改编成歌曲。她写了几首，她认为只要有一点时间一点帮助她就能做好。

00:48:16.340 --> 00:48:26.540
<v Vivien>I wouldn't say that I totally want Papa to be out of my career. As a singer, he's super supportive. And he's obviously the one with all the connections. But--
<v Vivien>我不会说我完全想让爸爸离开我的事业。作为歌手，他超级支持我。而且他显然是那个有所有关系的人。但——

00:48:26.540 --> 00:48:26.870
<v Vivien>[LAUGHTER]
<v Vivien>[大笑]

00:48:26.870 --> 00:48:29.240
<v Neil Drumming>Practical-- that was very practical of you. That's smart.
<v Neil Drumming>实际——你刚才太实际了。那很聪明。

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:31.580
<v Adam Mansbach>Yes. [LAUGHING] It's was very cold-blooded right there.
<v Adam Mansbach>是的。[大笑]那真是冷酷无情。

00:48:31.580 --> 00:48:38.740
<v Adam Mansbach>[LAUGHTER]
<v Adam Mansbach>[大笑]

00:48:38.740 --> 00:48:55.870
<v Neil Drumming>I'm a father myself. It's a new thing, so new, In fact, that all I can say definitively about the experience so far is that I am terrified every single day about what's coming. I look around to my friends, as scared people do. And the thing that Adam pulled off gives me some hope.
<v Neil Drumming>我自己也是个父亲。这是一件新鲜事，如此新鲜，事实上，到目前为止关于这段经历我能肯定的唯一一点是，我每天对即将到来的一切都感到恐惧。我和朋友们一样向四周望去，就像害怕的人那样。亚当完成的事给了我一些希望。

00:48:55.870 --> 00:49:13.750
<v Neil Drumming>He found an easy way to be with his daughter through difficult times. He constructed something fun that they could share, no matter what else was swirling around them. And the outcome was perhaps the best one that any parent can imagine-- Vivien got it. At least for a little while, she was right there with him.
<v Neil Drumming>他在困难时期找到了一种与女儿在一起的简单方式。他构建了一些有趣的东西，让他们可以分享，无论周围有什么东西在汹涌。结果可能是任何父母能想象到的最好的结果——维维安懂了。至少有一段时间，她就在他身边。

00:49:13.750 --> 00:49:20.070
<v Neil Drumming>What do you guys remember? Do you guys remember-- does anyone remember the super complicated song?
<v Neil Drumming>你们记得什么？你们记得——有人记得那首超级复杂的歌吗？

00:49:20.070 --> 00:49:24.600
<v Adam Mansbach>Yeah. [LAUGHING] I [INAUDIBLE].
<v Adam Mansbach>是的。[大笑]我[听不清]。

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:27.390
<v Vivien>I remember only the second verse, though.
<v Vivien>但我只记得第二段。

00:49:27.390 --> 00:49:27.990
<v Adam Mansbach>Oh, do it.
<v Adam Mansbach>哦，来吧。

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:33.100
<v Vivien>Wait. Let me just do it in my head for a second.
<v Vivien>等等。让我在脑子里过一遍。

00:49:33.100 --> 00:49:33.550
<v Adam Mansbach>Thank you.
<v Adam Mansbach>谢谢。

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:34.780
<v Vivien>You want me to do it now, Poppy?
<v Vivien>你想让我现在做吗，爸爸？

00:49:34.780 --> 00:49:36.220
<v Adam Mansbach>Yeah, do it.
<v Adam Mansbach>是的，做吧。

00:49:36.220 --> 00:49:49.400
<v Vivien>(RAPPING) [INAUDIBLE] solemnly swear to scar you horribly. Destiny calling me. Honestly rappers a bloodily body. They're haunting me. But they can't do much harm to me. I'm open like a pharmacy. Karmically, I'm untouchable, highly combustible. I can't remember the rest right now.
<v Vivien>(说唱) [听不清]庄严宣誓要给你留下可怕的伤疤。命运召唤我。老实说，说唱歌手们身体血淋淋的。他们在困扰我。但他们不能对我造成太多伤害。我像药房一样开放。业力上，我是不可触碰的，高度易燃的。我现在不记得其余的部分了。

00:49:49.400 --> 00:49:52.730
<v Vivien>[LAUGHTER]
<v Vivien>[大笑]

00:49:52.730 --> 00:49:55.970
<v Adam Mansbach>The Jazz Wolf is back. What?
<v Adam Mansbach>爵士狼回来了。什么？

00:49:55.970 --> 00:50:02.630
<v Vivien>Stop.
<v Vivien>停。

00:50:02.630 --> 00:51:11.270
<v Ira Glass>Neil Drumming.
<v Ira Glass>尼尔·德鲁明。

00:51:11.270 --> 00:51:33.560
<v Starlee Kine>I know a lot about small talk, because I worked in an office. And there's a lot of small talk that goes on. There's all these different forms of it. There's water cooler small talk, and there's elevator small talk, which actually isn't even that bad. Because most people think elevator small talk is the worst kind. But actually, I think it's OK, because it's finite. You know it's going to end at a certain point--
<v Starlee Kine>我知道很多关于闲聊的事，因为我在办公室工作。有很多闲聊在进行。它有各种不同的形式。有饮水机闲聊，还有电梯闲聊，其实一点也不坏。因为大多数人认为电梯闲聊是最糟糕的一种。但其实，我认为还可以，因为它有限。你知道它在某个点会结束——

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:34.050
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:51:34.050 --> 00:51:41.540
<v Starlee Kine>--which I appreciate very much. And then there's even bathroom small talk where I work, which is the worst thing you're ever going to experience in your entire life.
<v Starlee Kine>——我非常感激这一点。然后还有我工作地方的洗手间闲聊，那绝对是你一生中会经历的最糟糕的事情。

00:51:41.540 --> 00:51:41.990
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:51:41.990 --> 00:51:51.510
<v Starlee Kine>They'll be people small-talking through the stalls. I actually have some audio of small talk to play for you.
<v Starlee Kine>人们会在隔间里闲聊。我其实有一些闲聊的音频要放给你听。

00:51:51.510 --> 00:52:08.760
<v Man>Coffee is on its way. Here is a special selection of the Viking blend. And I have to get back to work.
<v Man>咖啡在路上了。这是维京拼配的特别精选。我得回去工作了。

00:52:08.760 --> 00:52:11.520
<v Man>[LAUGHTER]
<v Man>[大笑]

00:52:11.520 --> 00:52:30.830
<v Starlee Kine>So that's what happened in my office. Almost every day, that seems to happen, actually. We know we're drinking coffee. It's being made. But for some reason, when you do small talk, when you're caught in the trap of small talk, you feel you just had to talk about what's immediately happening in front of you, even though it's so obvious. And it's not even his fault. You know what I mean? It just-- everyone does it.
<v Starlee Kine>所以那就是我办公室发生的事。几乎每天，看起来似乎都会发生。我们知道我们在喝咖啡。它正在被制作。但出于某种原因，当你进行闲聊，当你陷入闲聊的陷阱时，你感觉你不得不谈论眼前立即发生的事情，即使它是如此明显。这甚至不是他的错。你明白我的意思吗？它只是——每个人都这样做。

00:52:30.830 --> 00:52:31.300
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:51.780
<v Starlee Kine>And so I can't take it anymore. So I devised The Rundown to eliminate small talk forever from the world. I want it to be done with. The Rundown is what it's supposed to do. The goal Of the Rundown is it's supposed to turn conversations from the conversations you're supposed to be having to the conversations you want to be having. OK?
<v Starlee Kine>所以我再也受不了了。所以我设计了“简报”来永远从世界上消除闲聊。我希望它被终结。简报是它应该做的。简报的目标是它应该把你们应该进行的对话变成你们想要进行的对话。好吗？

00:52:51.780 --> 00:53:03.260
<v Starlee Kine>And so I also have an example of The Rundown. I went to a movie. And there was a ticket guy selling tickets. And I just started talking to him. So it can be done on anyone.
<v Starlee Kine>所以我也有一个简报的例子。我去看电影。有个售票员在卖票。我只是开始和他说话。所以它可以在任何人身上完成。

00:53:03.260 --> 00:53:05.190
<v Man>I had ramen noodles for breakfast.
<v Man>我早餐吃了拉面。

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:09.120
<v Starlee Kine>The $0.25 ones or did you pay-- or the fancy ones?
<v Starlee Kine>25美分的还是你买了——或者高档的？

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:13.500
<v Man>I didn't pay for them. My girlfriend bought them.
<v Man>我没付钱。我女朋友买的。

00:53:13.500 --> 00:53:29.340
<v Starlee Kine>OK, and so you're thinking small talk. You're going to go after the ramen noodles, right? Like, what kind of ramen noodles? What hot water, meat? Something like that-- but if you're doing The Rundown, you just seize on the most interesting part of the conversation, OK?
<v Starlee Kine>好吧，所以你在考虑闲聊，你要去抓拉面，对吧？比如，什么样的拉面？什么热水，肉？之类的——但如果你在做简报，你就只抓住对话中最有趣的部分，好吗？

00:53:29.340 --> 00:53:40.320
<v Starlee Kine>And so he did give me a very, very valuable clue. He gave me this girlfriend, wonderful girlfriend, character to work with, OK? From that, you can proceed to--
<v Starlee Kine>所以他确实给了我一个非常、非常有价值的线索。他给了我这个女朋友，美妙的女朋友，角色去处理，好吗？从那里，你可以继续——

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:42.240
<v Starlee Kine>Are you in love?
<v Starlee Kine>你恋爱了吗？

00:53:42.240 --> 00:53:43.750
<v Man>I love her.
<v Man>我爱她。

00:53:43.750 --> 00:53:46.020
<v Starlee Kine>We don't know if-- you're not in love?
<v Starlee Kine>我们不知道是否——你没恋爱？

00:53:46.020 --> 00:53:50.310
<v Man>Oh, well, I think, yeah. I am definitely in love.
<v Man>哦，嗯，我想，是的。我绝对恋爱了。

00:53:50.310 --> 00:53:54.320
<v Starlee Kine>OK, so that's a little bit more to go with, right? Now we know he's capable of love--
<v Starlee Kine>好吧，所以这多了一些东西可以聊，对吧？现在我们知道他有能力爱——

00:53:54.320 --> 00:53:54.840
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:53:54.840 --> 00:54:04.830
<v Starlee Kine>--which is a lot more than knowing that he just had ramen noodles, right? And from there, OK, the possibilities just open up wide. You can pretty much go for anything now. So then you just keep going.
<v Starlee Kine>——这比知道他只是吃了拉面要多得多，对吧？从那里，好吧，可能性大开。你现在几乎可以去聊任何事情了。所以你就继续聊。

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:08.120
<v Starlee Kine>How many one-night stands have you had?
<v Starlee Kine>你有过多少次一夜情？

00:54:08.120 --> 00:54:20.370
<v Man>Uh, well, I don't-- I know how many girls I've been with, but I don't-- I never really counted which of them are one-night stands exactly.
<v Man>呃，嗯，我不知道——我知道我和多少个女孩在一起过，但我没有——我从来没真正计算过她们中有多少人是一夜情。

00:54:20.370 --> 00:54:23.130
<v Starlee Kine>How many girls?
<v Starlee Kine>多少女孩？

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:24.300
<v Man>44 and 1/2.
<v Man>44个半。

00:54:24.300 --> 00:54:28.190
<v Starlee Kine>44 and 1/2. How many virgins?
<v Starlee Kine>44个半。多少个处女？

00:54:28.190 --> 00:54:32.870
<v Man>I don't know. Estimate-- roughly, probably four or five.
<v Man>我不知道。估计——大概，可能有四个或五个。

00:54:32.870 --> 00:54:33.270
<v Man>[LAUGHTER]
<v Man>[大笑]

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:45.150
<v Starlee Kine>OK, so you might think, how many virgins, is an inappropriate question. But I actually think it's the perfect fallback, if you have nothing else to ask. You know what I mean? Because--
<v Starlee Kine>好吧，所以你可能会想，多少个处女，是个不合适的问题。但我其实认为它是完美的后备，如果你没有别的话可问。你明白我的意思吗？因为——

00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:46.980
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:54:46.980 --> 00:54:59.220
<v Starlee Kine>--as you can see, it's totally OK. He doesn't mind telling you. People are just wait-- they love to talk about themselves, you know? And that's what people want. And they also like to be asked questions that they have answers for. He completely knows the answer to, how many virgins?
<v Starlee Kine>——正如你所看到的，这完全没问题。他不介意告诉你。人们只是等——他们喜欢谈论自己，你知道吗？这就是人们想要的。而且他们也喜欢被问到他们有答案的问题。他完全知道“多少个处女”的答案。

00:54:59.220 --> 00:55:03.420
<v Starlee Kine>So then they feel good about themselves. And they'll keep going. And from there, it's pretty smooth sailing.
<v Starlee Kine>所以他们对自己的感觉很好。而且他们会继续聊。从那里开始，就相当顺利了。

00:55:03.420 --> 00:55:05.910
<v Man>The first one that came along was for 2 and 1/2 years.
<v Man>第一个出现的是两年半。

00:55:05.910 --> 00:55:06.330
<v Starlee Kine>Really?
<v Starlee Kine>真的吗？

00:55:06.330 --> 00:55:08.080
<v Man>That was the first girl I was ever with, though.
<v Man>不过那是我交往的第一个女孩。

00:55:08.080 --> 00:55:08.910
<v Starlee Kine>That was?
<v Starlee Kine>那是？

00:55:08.910 --> 00:55:24.090
<v Man>She was a virgin, too. We were both virgins. It was good, you know, but it was weird. It was kind of like an all day, all-around-town sort of thing. Because it started at her grandparents' house, which is in this all yellow room. The sheets were yellow. The window curtains were yellow.
<v Man>她也是处女。我们俩都是处女。这很好，你知道，但这很奇怪。它有点像是一整天、全城转的那种事情。因为它始于她祖父母的家，那在一个全黄色的房间里。床单是黄色的。窗帘是黄色的。

00:55:24.090 --> 00:55:35.100
<v Man>And then it just wasn't working out. So then we drove out to this spot by a drive-in movie screen that was abandoned. But it was a good experience altogether. I enjoyed it.
<v Man>然后它进展不顺。所以我们就开车去了那个废弃的汽车电影院屏幕旁的地方。但总的来说是一次很好的体验。我很享受。

00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:54.990
<v Starlee Kine>So that was a minute and 26 seconds. And now, after a minute and 26, through The Rundown, I know what he's had for breakfast, that he's capable of love, how many girls he slept with, how many of them were virgins, who he lost his-- first lost his virginity to, and even the color wallpaper of her grandmother's house. So that's pretty good. That's a pretty successful Rundown.
<v Starlee Kine>所以那是一分钟26秒。现在，一分钟26秒之后，通过简报，我知道了他早餐吃了什么，他有能力去爱，他睡过多少女孩，其中多少个是处女，谁是他第一次失去——第一次失去童贞的人，甚至她祖母房子的壁纸颜色。所以这很不错。那是一个相当成功的简报。

00:55:54.990 --> 00:56:10.870
<v Starlee Kine>And there's a few simple rules for everyone to be able to do it just like this eventually. So the first rule is, number one, small talk is a conversation you're supposed to be having. And The Rundown is a conversation you want to be having. Right?
<v Starlee Kine>每个人最终都能像这样做到这一点有几个简单的规则。所以第一条规则是，第一，闲聊是你应该进行的对话。而简报是你想要进行的对话。对吧？

00:56:10.870 --> 00:56:14.950
<v Starlee Kine>Number two is, why chew the fat, when you can chew the meat? OK?
<v Starlee Kine>第二条是，当你可以咀嚼肉的时候，为什么还要嚼那些肥肉呢？好吗？

00:56:14.950 --> 00:56:17.310
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:56:17.310 --> 00:56:22.290
<v Starlee Kine>Also very important. Number three is, if you can think it, you can ask it.
<v Starlee Kine>这也非常重要。第三条是，如果你能想到，你就能问出来。

00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:24.090
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:56:24.090 --> 00:56:33.090
<v Starlee Kine>OK? And then, if all else fails, or even if all else doesn't fail, number four is, how many virgins?
<v Starlee Kine>好吗？然后，如果一切都失败了，或者即使一切都没失败，第四条是，多少个处女？

00:56:33.090 --> 00:56:35.890
<v Starlee Kine>[LAUGHTER]
<v Starlee Kine>[大笑]

00:56:35.890 --> 00:56:38.340
<v Starlee Kine>So that's the run down.
<v Starlee Kine>所以那就是简报。

00:56:38.340 --> 00:56:43.110
<v Starlee Kine>[APPLAUSE AND CHEERING]
<v Starlee Kine>[掌声和欢呼]

00:56:43.110 --> 00:56:55.430
<v Ira Glass>Starlee Kine, speaking at The Little Gray Book Lectures, at the Galapagos Art Space, in New York. She writes for the HBO Max TV series "Search Party.":
<v Ira Glass>斯塔莉·凯恩，在纽约加拉帕戈斯艺术空间的“小灰书讲座”上发言。她为HBO Max电视系列剧“Search Party”撰稿。

